9
00:03:44,656 --> 00:03:46,359
<i>The next stop is Broad Street.</i>

10
00:03:46,391 --> 00:03:47,727
<i>Broad Street will be next.</i>

11
00:04:59,432 --> 00:05:00,666
Upstairs. This way.

12
00:05:10,009 --> 00:05:11,610
He didn't come up this way?
Anything?

13
00:05:11,644 --> 00:05:13,413
He's not up here.
Go back down.

14
00:05:15,848 --> 00:05:17,350
Excuse me.
Excuse me.

15
00:05:36,601 --> 00:05:38,670
Go ahead and park
around the corner.

16
00:06:13,004 --> 00:06:14,005
<font color="#80FF00">(CAR HONKS)</font>

17
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:35,588
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

18
00:08:25,204 --> 00:08:26,738
<font color="#80FF00">(TIRES SCREECH)</font>

19
00:08:51,163 --> 00:08:53,665
Visitors.

20
00:08:53,698 --> 00:08:55,067
Do you mind
if I fetch my teeth?

21
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:57,036
Colonel, would you
turn around, please?

22
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:01,139
Sit down on the bed.

23
00:09:01,173 --> 00:09:02,642
Let's go.

24
00:09:09,649 --> 00:09:11,216
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>

25
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:13,985
Uh, they're in the...
The teeth are in the... on the sink.

26
00:09:14,019 --> 00:09:15,855
Look at me. We are agents

27
00:09:15,888 --> 00:09:17,990
from the Federal Bureau
of Investigation.

28
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:19,091
Look at me!

29
00:09:19,125 --> 00:09:20,893
I'm talking to you.

30
00:09:20,927 --> 00:09:22,727
We've received information

31
00:09:22,761 --> 00:09:25,030
concerning your
involvement in espionage.

32
00:09:25,063 --> 00:09:27,066
You can either cooperate
with us right now,

33
00:09:27,099 --> 00:09:28,701
or you'll be under arrest.

34
00:09:28,734 --> 00:09:29,668
Do you understand, Colonel?

35
00:09:29,701 --> 00:09:31,636
No, not really.

36
00:09:31,671 --> 00:09:34,974
Why do you
keep calling me "Colonel"?

37
00:09:35,006 --> 00:09:36,775
<font color="#ffff00">BLASCO</font>: You need
to get dressed.

38
00:09:36,808 --> 00:09:38,844
We have to search
your apartment.

39
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:42,948
Would you mind if I, uh,
cleaned my palette?

40
00:09:42,981 --> 00:09:45,784
The paints,
it'll get ruined otherwise.

41
00:09:45,817 --> 00:09:47,153
It's just behind you there.

42
00:09:49,154 --> 00:09:51,123
I have a cloth myself.

43
00:09:51,157 --> 00:09:52,224
Thank you.

44
00:09:52,258 --> 00:09:54,994
All right, start searching the place.
Thank you.

45
00:09:55,027 --> 00:09:56,162
All right,
search the mattress,

46
00:09:56,195 --> 00:09:57,429
the bed, the floorboards,
his chair.

47
00:09:57,462 --> 00:09:59,731
Would you put that
in the ashtray on the windowsill there?

48
00:09:59,764 --> 00:10:01,299
<font color="#ffff00">BLASCO</font>: Take a good look
at that desk.

49
00:10:05,104 --> 00:10:06,639
<font color="#ffff00">FBI AGENT</font>: Let's see the desk.

50
00:10:13,312 --> 00:10:14,980
Wouldn't want
to get this ruined.

51
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:31,697
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>:
<i>Don't say "my guy."</i>

52
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:34,734
<i>He's not "my guy."</i>

53
00:10:34,767 --> 00:10:37,802
<font color="#ffff00">BATES</font>: Yes, he's "your guy."
Who are we talking about?

54
00:10:37,836 --> 00:10:41,774
We're talking about a guy
who was insured by my client.

55
00:10:41,807 --> 00:10:43,775
So, don't make him "my guy."

56
00:10:44,777 --> 00:10:46,312
<font color="#ffff00">BATES</font>: Okay, fine.

57
00:10:46,345 --> 00:10:49,914
My point is, the guy
insured by your client,

58
00:10:49,948 --> 00:10:52,717
he doesn't deny that
any of these things happened.

59
00:10:52,751 --> 00:10:55,287
"These... These things"?

60
00:10:55,321 --> 00:10:57,956
<font color="#ffff00">BATES</font>: Yes,
these five things.

61
00:10:57,990 --> 00:10:59,759
Wait, hold it,
hold it, hold it.

62
00:10:59,792 --> 00:11:01,059
Not five things.

63
00:11:01,092 --> 00:11:03,062
One thing.

64
00:11:03,095 --> 00:11:04,196
<font color="#ffff00">BATES</font>: Clearly,
it's five things.

65
00:11:04,230 --> 00:11:05,965
Well, I'm sorry,
it's not clear to me.

66
00:11:05,998 --> 00:11:08,801
Five things?
Explain it to me.

67
00:11:08,833 --> 00:11:10,068
<font color="#ffff00">BATES</font>: It's self-evident.

68
00:11:10,101 --> 00:11:11,971
Okay, then tell me
what happened.

69
00:11:12,004 --> 00:11:15,341
Tell me the story in a way
that makes sense for five things.

70
00:11:15,374 --> 00:11:17,777
Fine, absolutely.
Your guy is...

71
00:11:17,809 --> 00:11:19,144
Not my guy.
Insured by my client.

72
00:11:19,177 --> 00:11:20,812
The guy insured
by your client

73
00:11:20,846 --> 00:11:22,313
is driving down
State Highway 19

74
00:11:22,347 --> 00:11:24,950
when he loses control
of his car and hits my five guys,

75
00:11:24,984 --> 00:11:27,086
the five guys who hired me
to represent them

76
00:11:27,119 --> 00:11:28,553
because you're not
honoring your claim.

77
00:11:28,586 --> 00:11:32,290
You mean my client is not
honoring the claim, the insurance company?

78
00:11:32,323 --> 00:11:34,960
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font> Mr. Donovan,
we're all clear on who's who, here.

79
00:11:34,994 --> 00:11:37,797
Except my client
honors every claim.

80
00:11:37,830 --> 00:11:38,931
They do, Mr. Bates.

81
00:11:38,963 --> 00:11:40,298
Every single
legitimate claim,

82
00:11:40,331 --> 00:11:41,966
up to the limit
of their liability,

83
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,135
which is $100,000
per accident,

84
00:11:44,169 --> 00:11:46,304
in the case of
this man's policy.

85
00:11:46,337 --> 00:11:47,505
And this is one claim.

86
00:11:47,539 --> 00:11:51,243
According to your description,
"He hit my five guys."

87
00:11:51,277 --> 00:11:54,279
The guy insured by my client
had one accident.

88
00:11:54,312 --> 00:11:55,548
One, one, one.

89
00:11:55,581 --> 00:11:59,118
Losing control of the car and
hitting five motorcyclists.

90
00:11:59,150 --> 00:12:01,352
From their point of view,
five things happened.

91
00:12:02,421 --> 00:12:05,224
Well, look, Bob.
May I? Bob?

92
00:12:06,225 --> 00:12:08,326
"Jim."

93
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,129
If I go bowling and I throw
a strike, one thing happened.

94
00:12:11,163 --> 00:12:12,230
Ten things didn't happen.

95
00:12:12,264 --> 00:12:14,299
Jim, my guys
aren't bowling pins,

96
00:12:14,332 --> 00:12:16,034
as much as your guy
may have treated them as such.

97
00:12:16,067 --> 00:12:17,369
If... Let me finish.
Let me finish.

98
00:12:17,403 --> 00:12:20,206
If your house
is insured for $100,000

99
00:12:20,239 --> 00:12:22,240
and a tornado
carries it away,

100
00:12:22,274 --> 00:12:23,942
it carried away one house.

101
00:12:23,975 --> 00:12:25,411
It didn't pick up
every stick of furniture

102
00:12:25,443 --> 00:12:28,114
and destroy it
in a separate incident.

103
00:12:28,147 --> 00:12:30,883
If that is
what you're saying,

104
00:12:30,916 --> 00:12:34,153
well, then there is never
any limit to our liability

105
00:12:34,186 --> 00:12:37,188
and that is the end
of the insurance business.

106
00:12:37,222 --> 00:12:39,390
And then, Bob,
nobody is safe.

107
00:12:45,096 --> 00:12:47,298
Watters, Cowan and Donovan.
How may I direct your call?

108
00:12:47,332 --> 00:12:48,967
Morning, Mr. Donovan.

109
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,302
Good morning, Alison.

110
00:12:50,336 --> 00:12:52,270
I moved your 9:00 a.m.
with Prudential, sir.

111
00:12:52,303 --> 00:12:53,404
Why did you do that?

112
00:12:53,438 --> 00:12:55,006
I asked her to.

113
00:12:55,975 --> 00:12:57,275
Is everything
all right, Tom?

114
00:12:57,308 --> 00:12:59,979
Why don't you leave your stuff with Alison.
Come on inside.

115
00:13:01,147 --> 00:13:02,413
Something pretty important
has come up.

116
00:13:02,447 --> 00:13:04,182
Somebody's here to see us.

117
00:13:04,216 --> 00:13:06,051
Natalie's getting
your coffee.

118
00:13:06,085 --> 00:13:08,854
Nescafe, cream, two lumps,
right?

119
00:13:08,888 --> 00:13:10,155
That's right. Thanks.

120
00:13:12,224 --> 00:13:13,424
Lynn!
Jim!

121
00:13:13,458 --> 00:13:15,326
Nice to see you again.
How's the family?

122
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:17,163
Great, good. Thanks.
How's Annie?

123
00:13:17,196 --> 00:13:19,431
Homicidal. We had
the in-laws for the holiday.

124
00:13:19,465 --> 00:13:21,332
Well, if she acts on it,
she'll have good representation.

125
00:13:21,366 --> 00:13:22,467
<font color="#80FF00">(LAUGHS)</font>

126
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:24,036
Now, what's up?

127
00:13:30,475 --> 00:13:32,378
Okay, here's the thing.

128
00:13:32,411 --> 00:13:35,914
The Soviet spy they caught,
we want you to defend him.

129
00:13:35,947 --> 00:13:37,216
Here's the indictment.

130
00:13:40,418 --> 00:13:41,419
Wow.

131
00:13:43,288 --> 00:13:45,157
I'm not sure
I wanna pick that up.

132
00:13:45,191 --> 00:13:47,225
The accused
doesn't know any lawyers.

133
00:13:47,259 --> 00:13:49,195
The federal court
tossed it into our lap.

134
00:13:50,495 --> 00:13:52,431
The Bar Committee took a vote.

135
00:13:52,463 --> 00:13:54,265
You're the unanimous choice.

136
00:13:54,299 --> 00:13:55,900
It was important to us...

137
00:13:55,934 --> 00:13:57,235
It's important
to our country, Jim,

138
00:13:57,269 --> 00:13:59,205
that this man is seen
as getting a fair shake.

139
00:13:59,238 --> 00:14:01,240
American justice
will be on trial.

140
00:14:01,273 --> 00:14:03,174
Well, of course,
when you put it that way,

141
00:14:03,208 --> 00:14:05,177
it's an honor to be asked.

142
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:07,111
But, Lynn,
I'm an insurance lawyer.

143
00:14:07,145 --> 00:14:09,081
I haven't done
criminal work in years.

144
00:14:09,114 --> 00:14:11,115
It's like riding a bike,
isn't it?

145
00:14:11,149 --> 00:14:13,219
You distinguished yourself
at Nuremberg.

146
00:14:13,251 --> 00:14:14,953
I was on
the prosecution team.

147
00:14:14,986 --> 00:14:16,221
Not the point.

148
00:14:16,254 --> 00:14:17,615
You're no stranger
to criminal law.

149
00:14:18,356 --> 00:14:20,292
Jim, look at the situation.

150
00:14:20,326 --> 00:14:22,360
The man is publicly reviled.

151
00:14:22,394 --> 00:14:23,394
And I will be, too.

152
00:14:23,428 --> 00:14:25,264
Yes, in more
ignorant quarters.

153
00:14:25,297 --> 00:14:27,131
But that's exactly why
this has to be done,

154
00:14:27,166 --> 00:14:29,235
and capably done.

155
00:14:29,268 --> 00:14:31,236
It can't look like
our justice system

156
00:14:31,269 --> 00:14:32,429
tosses people
on the ash heap.

157
00:14:37,976 --> 00:14:40,245
Suppose I did,
what's the evidence look like?

158
00:14:41,513 --> 00:14:43,014
Pretty overwhelming.

159
00:14:43,514 --> 00:14:45,517
Great. Great.

160
00:14:45,551 --> 00:14:48,187
Everyone will hate me
but at least I'll lose.

161
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,322
<font color="#80FF00">(BOTH CHUCKLE)</font>

162
00:14:50,355 --> 00:14:52,423
Well, what do
you think, Tom?

163
00:14:52,457 --> 00:14:55,126
The firm will suffer.
I have work here.

164
00:14:55,159 --> 00:14:57,196
I think
it's a patriotic duty.

165
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:59,197
It's an important mission.

166
00:14:59,230 --> 00:15:01,432
I told Lynn,
the firm can't say no,

167
00:15:01,466 --> 00:15:03,534
and you'd have a tough time
saying no, too.

168
00:15:05,136 --> 00:15:08,006
I think you have to defend
the son of a bitch.

169
00:15:08,039 --> 00:15:10,042
So, you're doing it?

170
00:15:10,075 --> 00:15:11,409
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Doing what?

171
00:15:11,442 --> 00:15:14,446
Defending Abel,
the Soviet spy.

172
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:15,780
I may. I have to speak to...

173
00:15:15,813 --> 00:15:18,450
Wait, how do you know?
Did they post it on the bulletin board?

174
00:15:18,484 --> 00:15:20,552
No, I... I was
talking to Richard Earl...

175
00:15:20,586 --> 00:15:22,488
Look, if I do this,
I'm going to need your help.

176
00:15:24,289 --> 00:15:26,024
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES NERVOUSLY)</font>

177
00:15:26,057 --> 00:15:27,293
Can you work tonight?

178
00:15:29,161 --> 00:15:30,400
Well, I have
a dinner date, sir.

179
00:15:34,299 --> 00:15:36,302
It's Tuesday, right?
Yeah, no, I'm free, completely free.

180
00:15:36,335 --> 00:15:37,436
Thank you, sir.

181
00:15:41,472 --> 00:15:43,308
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: But it's also an honor.

182
00:15:43,341 --> 00:15:45,443
And the Bar Association
asked me

183
00:15:45,476 --> 00:15:47,245
because they want
to show that even a spy

184
00:15:47,278 --> 00:15:49,048
gets a capable advocate.

185
00:15:49,081 --> 00:15:51,216
Well, maybe it's the kind of honor
we could do without.

186
00:15:51,250 --> 00:15:52,649
Hey, honey,
what are you doing home?

187
00:15:54,052 --> 00:15:55,052
I got stood up.

188
00:15:56,120 --> 00:15:58,557
Well, that's despicable.

189
00:15:58,590 --> 00:16:00,158
Who is this knot-head?

190
00:16:00,192 --> 00:16:01,360
I'd rather not say.

191
00:16:01,393 --> 00:16:03,528
<font color="#ffff00">MARY</font>: Well, sit down.
We just started.

192
00:16:03,562 --> 00:16:04,730
People are scared.

193
00:16:04,763 --> 00:16:06,597
They're building
bomb shelters to protect themselves

194
00:16:06,631 --> 00:16:09,033
from people like this man.

195
00:16:09,067 --> 00:16:10,235
I go to the store,

196
00:16:10,269 --> 00:16:13,572
people are buying
canned food and potassium tablets.

197
00:16:13,605 --> 00:16:16,408
It's all about this man
and what he represents.

198
00:16:16,442 --> 00:16:18,509
He's a threat to all of us.
A traitor.

199
00:16:18,543 --> 00:16:20,346
<font color="#ffff00">ROGER</font>: Who's a traitor?
The Rosenbergs were traitors.

200
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:21,279
Who were they?

201
00:16:21,312 --> 00:16:23,381
They gave atomic secrets
to the Russians.

202
00:16:23,414 --> 00:16:25,384
They were Americans.
They betrayed their country.

203
00:16:25,417 --> 00:16:27,485
You can't accuse Abel
of being a traitor.

204
00:16:27,519 --> 00:16:29,053
He's not an American.

205
00:16:29,087 --> 00:16:30,456
<font color="#80FF00">(SOFTLY)</font>
Oh, listen to yourself.

206
00:16:30,489 --> 00:16:32,124
You're defending him already.

207
00:16:32,157 --> 00:16:33,192
You're rehearsing it on me.

208
00:16:33,225 --> 00:16:35,226
You said you were
just thinking about taking it.

209
00:16:35,259 --> 00:16:38,129
I am just thinking about it,
just very hard.

210
00:16:38,163 --> 00:16:41,100
Everyone deserves a defense.
Every person matters.

211
00:16:41,133 --> 00:16:42,433
Jim, what do we deserve?

212
00:16:42,467 --> 00:16:44,403
Do you know how
people will look at us?

213
00:16:44,436 --> 00:16:46,605
The family of a man
trying to free a traitor?

214
00:16:46,638 --> 00:16:48,539
He's not a traitor, Mary.
Yes, yes.

215
00:16:48,573 --> 00:16:50,109
<font color="#80FF00">(PHONE RINGING)</font>
Roger, get that! He's not a traitor...

216
00:16:50,142 --> 00:16:51,376
Why do I have to do
all the work?

217
00:16:51,409 --> 00:16:53,211
Oh! You wait till we say grace.
I'm hungry!

218
00:16:53,245 --> 00:16:54,613
He's about the most unpopular
man in this country,

219
00:16:54,645 --> 00:16:56,248
and you're trying
to take second place!

220
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:57,682
Yeah, and I'm third.

221
00:16:57,716 --> 00:16:59,684
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Carol, you just dated the wrong guy.
I wanna eat.

222
00:16:59,717 --> 00:17:01,253
Mary, don't make
this an argument

223
00:17:01,285 --> 00:17:02,287
when we're not having
an argument.

224
00:17:02,321 --> 00:17:03,589
You're against him,
I'm for him?

225
00:17:03,621 --> 00:17:06,424
No, I'm not for him.
I'm not for the Russians spying on us.

226
00:17:06,458 --> 00:17:09,428
I'm for his right to have a defense
in a court of law,

227
00:17:09,461 --> 00:17:11,462
which is why they call it
a court of law.

228
00:17:11,497 --> 00:17:13,365
I didn't mean
to interrupt dinner.

229
00:17:13,398 --> 00:17:14,599
A court of law...
Hey, Doug.

230
00:17:14,633 --> 00:17:19,304
Oh, no, not at all.
Hello, Doug. Jim was just flailing.

231
00:17:19,337 --> 00:17:21,573
I'm not flailing.
Go ahead, Doug, you can join us for dinner.

232
00:17:21,606 --> 00:17:23,409
We're having
meatloaf tonight.

233
00:17:25,676 --> 00:17:28,212
So, you came over
to help Jim think about

234
00:17:28,247 --> 00:17:29,448
whether he's
taking the case?

235
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:32,351
Oh, we're taking it!
It's exciting, isn't it?

236
00:17:34,252 --> 00:17:35,253
<font color="#80FF00">(SIGHS)</font>

237
00:17:40,459 --> 00:17:42,193
Lord, we thank thee
for thy blessings

238
00:17:42,227 --> 00:17:43,628
we are about to receive.

239
00:17:43,662 --> 00:17:46,198
Thy bounty through Christ,
our Lord. Amen.

240
00:17:53,638 --> 00:17:55,740
Good morning, sir.
My name is Jim Donovan.

241
00:17:55,773 --> 00:17:57,742
These are my credentials.

242
00:17:57,776 --> 00:18:00,211
I am a partner in
Watters, Cowan and Donovan.

243
00:18:00,244 --> 00:18:03,615
I was admitted to
the New York Bar in 1941.

244
00:18:03,648 --> 00:18:06,117
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR BANGING)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(CHAIN RATTLING)</font>

245
00:18:07,653 --> 00:18:11,522
You have been charged
with three counts and 19 overt acts.

246
00:18:11,557 --> 00:18:14,226
Conspiracy to transmit
United States defense

247
00:18:14,259 --> 00:18:15,760
and atomic secrets
to the Soviet Union,

248
00:18:15,794 --> 00:18:17,528
conspiracy to
gather secrets,

249
00:18:17,563 --> 00:18:19,698
and failing to register
as a foreign agent.

250
00:18:25,570 --> 00:18:27,739
Do many foreign
agents register?

251
00:18:32,778 --> 00:18:35,247
If you don't mind
my asking, sir,

252
00:18:36,548 --> 00:18:39,484
since your arrest,
where have you been?

253
00:18:43,221 --> 00:18:44,455
I couldn't say for certain.

254
00:18:46,825 --> 00:18:47,825
You don't know?

255
00:18:49,528 --> 00:18:51,395
They drove me to an airport

256
00:18:51,430 --> 00:18:53,565
and put me on a plane,
took me off the plane.

257
00:18:55,701 --> 00:18:57,236
Somewhere hot.

258
00:18:58,436 --> 00:18:59,471
It's hot here.

259
00:18:59,505 --> 00:19:02,441
Hotter, very humid.
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

260
00:19:02,473 --> 00:19:04,409
They put me in a room.

261
00:19:06,345 --> 00:19:07,645
Were you beaten?

262
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:09,681
No.

263
00:19:09,714 --> 00:19:11,682
I was talked to.

264
00:19:11,717 --> 00:19:13,785
Offers were made.

265
00:19:13,818 --> 00:19:14,886
What do you mean?

266
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:18,423
Offers of employment,
to work for your government.

267
00:19:20,726 --> 00:19:23,362
I was told if I cooperated,

268
00:19:23,394 --> 00:19:25,564
no further charges
would be made against me.

269
00:19:26,865 --> 00:19:28,400
And I would be given money.

270
00:19:29,734 --> 00:19:30,836
Hmm.

271
00:19:30,868 --> 00:19:32,436
And you declined?

272
00:19:33,838 --> 00:19:36,307
As you see.

273
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:38,710
Well, of course
I can't endorse that.

274
00:19:38,743 --> 00:19:40,679
I feel duty-bound
to urge you

275
00:19:40,711 --> 00:19:42,814
to cooperate with
the U.S. government.

276
00:19:42,847 --> 00:19:44,249
Well, I said no.

277
00:19:44,282 --> 00:19:47,486
Perhaps you could relay
to your friends at the CIA that I meant it.

278
00:19:47,519 --> 00:19:49,520
No, no, no.

279
00:19:49,555 --> 00:19:51,490
I... I don't work
for the agency.

280
00:19:53,392 --> 00:19:55,460
I don't work
for the government.

281
00:19:55,493 --> 00:19:58,730
I am here to offer my services
as your legal counsel.

282
00:19:58,763 --> 00:20:01,599
If you accept them
as such,

283
00:20:02,768 --> 00:20:04,368
I work for you.

284
00:20:04,903 --> 00:20:06,837
If I accept you?

285
00:20:06,872 --> 00:20:08,406
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLES)</font>

286
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:09,575
<font color="#80FF00">(SWALLOWS)</font>

287
00:20:13,478 --> 00:20:15,446
Are you good at what you do?

288
00:20:16,914 --> 00:20:19,483
Yeah.
Yeah, I'm pretty good.

289
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:25,590
Have you represented
many accused spies?

290
00:20:26,857 --> 00:20:28,492
<font color="#80FF00">(INHALES)</font>

291
00:20:28,527 --> 00:20:30,295
No, not yet.

292
00:20:31,762 --> 00:20:34,398
This will be a first
for the both of us.

293
00:20:36,667 --> 00:20:37,903
<font color="#80FF00">(LAUGHS)</font>

294
00:20:37,935 --> 00:20:39,337
Yes.

295
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:43,407
All right.

296
00:20:43,442 --> 00:20:44,875
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: "All right"?
You accept?

297
00:20:47,511 --> 00:20:48,846
Yes, all right.

298
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:51,383
Good, okay.

299
00:20:51,415 --> 00:20:52,550
Let's start here.

300
00:20:52,584 --> 00:20:53,719
If you are firm
in your resolve

301
00:20:53,751 --> 00:20:55,354
not to cooperate
with the U.S. government...

302
00:20:55,386 --> 00:20:56,488
I am.

303
00:20:56,520 --> 00:20:57,923
Yeah.

304
00:20:57,955 --> 00:20:59,790
Then do not talk
to anybody else about your case,

305
00:20:59,825 --> 00:21:01,526
inside of government or out.

306
00:21:01,559 --> 00:21:04,663
Except to me,
to the extent that you trust me.

307
00:21:04,695 --> 00:21:06,698
I have a mandate to serve you.
Nobody else does.

308
00:21:06,731 --> 00:21:08,500
Quite frankly,
everybody else has an interest

309
00:21:08,532 --> 00:21:09,972
in sending you
to the electric chair.

310
00:21:10,669 --> 00:21:11,669
All right.

311
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:16,642
You don't seem alarmed.

312
00:21:18,977 --> 00:21:20,545
<font color="#80FF00">(MUMBLES)</font>

313
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:23,582
Would it help?

314
00:21:33,858 --> 00:21:37,929
I... I would like
materials to draw with.

315
00:21:37,962 --> 00:21:39,431
That's not possible.

316
00:21:40,464 --> 00:21:42,401
A pencil, a piece of paper.

317
00:21:42,433 --> 00:21:43,634
Cigarettes.

318
00:21:45,971 --> 00:21:47,605
Please?

319
00:21:55,012 --> 00:21:57,816
Mr. Donovan,
you have men like me

320
00:21:57,848 --> 00:22:00,419
doing the same
for your country.

322
00:22:01,522 --> 00:22:06,825
If they were caught,
I'm sure that you would
wish them to be treated well.

323
00:22:11,496 --> 00:22:12,763
<font color="#ffff00">CIA AGENT</font>: What's your name?

324
00:22:12,798 --> 00:22:14,432
Francis Gary Powers.

325
00:22:16,634 --> 00:22:18,470
<font color="#ffff00">CIA AGENT</font>: Rank, service?

326
00:22:18,502 --> 00:22:20,705
First Lieutenant,
United States Air Force.

327
00:22:22,773 --> 00:22:24,476
Do you have any association

328
00:22:24,509 --> 00:22:26,510
with the Soviet Union,
Lieutenant Powers?

329
00:22:26,545 --> 00:22:27,746
Kidding?

330
00:22:27,778 --> 00:22:28,946
Just answer the questions,
Lieutenant.

331
00:22:28,980 --> 00:22:31,549
Yes, no,
or as simply as you can.

332
00:22:31,583 --> 00:22:33,751
Do you have any association
with the Soviet Union?

333
00:22:33,785 --> 00:22:35,487
Hell, no.

334
00:22:37,055 --> 00:22:38,490
Do you know
why you're here?

335
00:22:38,522 --> 00:22:39,691
<font color="#ffff00">POWERS</font>: Mmm-mmm.

336
00:22:40,625 --> 00:22:42,794
Is that a no?

337
00:22:42,827 --> 00:22:45,029
Yes. I mean...

338
00:22:45,063 --> 00:22:46,932
No, it's just "no."

339
00:22:46,964 --> 00:22:49,500
Yes, I have absolutely
no idea why I'm here.

340
00:22:49,534 --> 00:22:50,701
Eyes front, Lieutenant.

341
00:22:50,736 --> 00:22:51,869
Yes, sir.

342
00:23:09,621 --> 00:23:10,855
How'd you do in there, Gary?

343
00:23:12,691 --> 00:23:15,594
Pretty good.
Think I got my name right.

344
00:23:15,626 --> 00:23:18,563
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font> You probably
outscored Shinn, then.

345
00:23:18,596 --> 00:23:19,998
<font color="#80FF00">(ALL CHUCKLING)</font>

346
00:23:20,030 --> 00:23:21,465
Who's winning?

347
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:22,733
Uh, that would be me.

348
00:23:22,768 --> 00:23:24,702
Oh. Well, deal me in.

349
00:23:24,736 --> 00:23:26,938
<font color="#ffff00">JOE</font>: So, these are
all mine, then.

350
00:23:26,971 --> 00:23:28,807
<font color="#ffff00">POWERS</font>: I told you boys
not to play with him.

351
00:23:30,608 --> 00:23:31,809
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR CLOSES)</font>

352
00:23:40,852 --> 00:23:41,952
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

353
00:23:51,997 --> 00:23:53,698
<font color="#80FF00">(BREATHES DEEPLY)</font>

354
00:23:55,834 --> 00:23:57,669
Okay, drivers,
here's the deal.

355
00:23:59,136 --> 00:24:01,006
You've been selected
for a mission

356
00:24:01,038 --> 00:24:02,574
which you are
not to discuss

357
00:24:02,606 --> 00:24:04,875
with anyone
outside of this room.

358
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:06,644
No one.

359
00:24:06,678 --> 00:24:08,712
I don't care who you trust.

360
00:24:08,747 --> 00:24:10,714
Wife, mother, sweetheart.

361
00:24:10,749 --> 00:24:12,717
The good Lord
when you pray at night.

362
00:24:12,750 --> 00:24:14,685
You don't tell
any of them anything

363
00:24:14,719 --> 00:24:16,520
of what
I'm about to tell you.

364
00:24:19,023 --> 00:24:21,759
Each of you drivers has
met certain qualifications.

365
00:24:22,626 --> 00:24:24,829
High level
security clearance.

366
00:24:24,863 --> 00:24:26,631
Exceptional pilot ratings

367
00:24:26,664 --> 00:24:28,633
in excess of
the required hours flight time

368
00:24:28,666 --> 00:24:30,602
in a single-seat aircraft.

369
00:24:30,634 --> 00:24:33,605
We are engaged in a war.

370
00:24:33,637 --> 00:24:35,874
This war does not, for the moment,
involve men-at-arms.

371
00:24:35,906 --> 00:24:37,776
It involves information.

372
00:24:37,808 --> 00:24:40,010
You will be
collecting information.

373
00:24:40,045 --> 00:24:42,881
You will be gathering
intelligence about the enemy.

374
00:24:42,913 --> 00:24:46,550
The intelligence you gather
could give us the upper hand

375
00:24:46,585 --> 00:24:49,119
in a full thermonuclear exchange
with the Soviet Union.

376
00:24:51,155 --> 00:24:52,590
Or it could prevent one.

377
00:24:53,924 --> 00:24:56,126
For public purposes,

378
00:24:56,161 --> 00:24:57,929
as far as your wife or mother

379
00:24:57,962 --> 00:24:59,931
or sweetheart
or the good Lord above,

380
00:24:59,964 --> 00:25:01,967
your mission does not exist.

381
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,836
If it does not exist,
you do not exist.

382
00:25:04,869 --> 00:25:07,972
You cannot be shot down.
You cannot be captured.

383
00:25:14,712 --> 00:25:16,047
You work for the CIA now.

384
00:25:26,691 --> 00:25:28,926
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR CLOSES)</font>

385
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:32,797
So, Jim, I heard your guy is
speaking with a phony accent.

386
00:25:33,898 --> 00:25:35,901
Does he keep that up
all the time?

387
00:25:35,933 --> 00:25:38,670
Actually, I'm pretty sure
that's just the way he talks.

388
00:25:38,702 --> 00:25:40,805
He's got the Russian name
but a British passport.

389
00:25:40,838 --> 00:25:43,741
Well, I doubt
that that's genuine.

390
00:25:43,775 --> 00:25:46,044
So, Jim, where are we?
I see here...

392
00:25:46,071 --> 00:25:53,084
Yes, uh, Judge, you see,
I just don't think that three weeks
is going to do it, here.

393
00:25:53,117 --> 00:25:55,086
We... We got a massive amount
of evidence, as you know.

394
00:25:55,119 --> 00:25:56,186
You wanna postpone?

395
00:25:56,221 --> 00:25:57,388
Six weeks.

396
00:25:57,422 --> 00:26:00,892
I mean, there's just myself
and my associate, basically.

397
00:26:00,925 --> 00:26:02,126
Jim, is this serious?

398
00:26:02,160 --> 00:26:03,161
Sir?

399
00:26:06,230 --> 00:26:07,999
Is this serious?

400
00:26:08,032 --> 00:26:09,667
Yes. Yes, indeed, it is.

401
00:26:09,701 --> 00:26:11,102
You can see
in the filing...

402
00:26:11,135 --> 00:26:13,971
Jim, this man is a Soviet spy.

403
00:26:14,005 --> 00:26:16,674
Allegedly.
Come on, Counselor!

404
00:26:18,242 --> 00:26:19,743
Your Honor.

405
00:26:19,778 --> 00:26:21,179
Of course, I salute you.

406
00:26:21,211 --> 00:26:23,748
We all salute you for
taking on a thankless task.

407
00:26:23,781 --> 00:26:25,650
This man has to have
due process.

408
00:26:25,683 --> 00:26:26,951
But let's not kid each other.

409
00:26:26,984 --> 00:26:28,118
<font color="#80FF00">(KNOCKING ON DOOR)</font>

410
00:26:28,153 --> 00:26:30,989
He'll receive
a capable defense.

411
00:26:31,021 --> 00:26:34,726
And, God willing,
he'll be convicted.

412
00:26:34,759 --> 00:26:36,126
Come on, Counselor.

413
00:26:36,161 --> 00:26:37,695
Let's not play games
with this.

414
00:26:37,729 --> 00:26:39,830
Not in my courtroom.

415
00:26:39,864 --> 00:26:41,865
We have a date

416
00:26:41,900 --> 00:26:43,734
and we're going to trial.

417
00:26:47,305 --> 00:26:48,640
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR CLOSES)</font>

418
00:26:52,911 --> 00:26:54,878
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Taxi!

419
00:26:54,913 --> 00:26:56,681
Taxi!

420
00:26:56,714 --> 00:26:59,683
I see your light on!
Son of a bitch.

421
00:27:01,051 --> 00:27:03,755
Can't wait to get back
to Manhattan.

422
00:27:03,788 --> 00:27:04,990
His light was on, right?

423
00:27:06,124 --> 00:27:07,959
Son of a bitch.

424
00:27:54,972 --> 00:27:56,807
<font color="#ffff00">MAN</font>: Against their
level of comfort.

425
00:27:56,840 --> 00:27:57,909
The statute hasn't changed.

426
00:27:57,942 --> 00:27:59,344
Oh, excuse me, sir.

427
00:27:59,376 --> 00:28:01,046
Pardon me. Sorry. Oh!

428
00:28:02,212 --> 00:28:04,048
<font color="#80FF00">(CHATTERING CONTINUES
INDISTINCTLY)</font>

429
00:28:32,376 --> 00:28:34,045
Mr. Donovan.

430
00:28:36,381 --> 00:28:38,048
What?

431
00:28:41,385 --> 00:28:42,987
What?

432
00:28:54,265 --> 00:28:55,266
<font color="#80FF00">(TUTTING)</font>

433
00:28:55,299 --> 00:28:57,268
CIA.

434
00:28:57,301 --> 00:28:58,301
Yeah.

435
00:28:59,203 --> 00:29:00,371
I just wanted to chat.

436
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:06,176
How's the case going?

437
00:29:06,211 --> 00:29:08,980
Case is going great.
Couldn't be better.

438
00:29:09,012 --> 00:29:10,080
Mmm-hmm.

439
00:29:10,981 --> 00:29:12,116
Has your guy talked?

440
00:29:16,453 --> 00:29:19,089
Excuse me.

441
00:29:19,124 --> 00:29:21,859
You met him. Has he talked?
Has he said anything yet?

442
00:29:24,128 --> 00:29:25,963
We're not having
this conversation.

443
00:29:25,997 --> 00:29:28,098
No, of course not.

444
00:29:28,133 --> 00:29:30,300
No, I mean,
we really are not having it.

445
00:29:30,335 --> 00:29:34,038
You're asking me to violate
attorney-client privilege.

446
00:29:34,072 --> 00:29:35,339
Oh, come on, Counselor.

447
00:29:35,373 --> 00:29:36,340
You know,
I wish people like you

448
00:29:36,374 --> 00:29:37,442
would quit saying
"Oh, come on, Counselor."

449
00:29:37,474 --> 00:29:39,344
I didn't like it the first
time it happened today.

450
00:29:39,376 --> 00:29:41,179
A judge said it to me twice.

451
00:29:41,211 --> 00:29:43,213
And the more I hear it,
the more I don't like it.

452
00:29:46,483 --> 00:29:49,086
Okay, well, listen,
I understand attorney-client privilege.

453
00:29:49,119 --> 00:29:52,323
I understand
all the legal gamesmanship.

454
00:29:52,356 --> 00:29:55,058
And I understand
that's how you make a living.

455
00:29:55,093 --> 00:29:57,961
But I'm talking to you
about something else.

456
00:29:59,963 --> 00:30:02,066
The security
of your country.

457
00:30:02,099 --> 00:30:05,236
And I'm sorry
if the way I put it offends you.

458
00:30:05,269 --> 00:30:08,940
But we need to know
what Abel is telling you.

459
00:30:08,972 --> 00:30:11,041
You understand me, Donovan?

460
00:30:11,075 --> 00:30:13,111
We need to know.

461
00:30:13,144 --> 00:30:14,913
Don't go "Boy Scout" on me.

462
00:30:16,381 --> 00:30:18,182
We don't have
a rule book, here.

463
00:30:19,384 --> 00:30:21,486
You're Agent Hoffman, yeah?

464
00:30:21,519 --> 00:30:23,387
Yeah.

465
00:30:23,421 --> 00:30:25,490
German extraction.

466
00:30:25,522 --> 00:30:26,958
Yeah, so?

467
00:30:26,990 --> 00:30:28,893
My name's Donovan. Irish.

468
00:30:28,926 --> 00:30:31,328
Both sides,
mother and father.

469
00:30:31,362 --> 00:30:34,499
I'm Irish, you're German.

470
00:30:34,531 --> 00:30:37,367
But what makes us
both Americans?

471
00:30:37,402 --> 00:30:39,470
Just one thing.

472
00:30:39,503 --> 00:30:41,138
One, one, one.

473
00:30:42,272 --> 00:30:43,272
The rule book.

474
00:30:44,409 --> 00:30:46,176
We call it the Constitution

475
00:30:46,211 --> 00:30:48,179
and we agree to the rules,

476
00:30:48,212 --> 00:30:49,980
and that's what
makes us Americans.

477
00:30:50,013 --> 00:30:51,148
It's all that
makes us Americans

478
00:30:51,182 --> 00:30:52,250
so don't tell me
there's no rule book,

479
00:30:52,282 --> 00:30:54,885
and don't nod at me like that,
you son of a bitch.

480
00:31:06,029 --> 00:31:07,432
Do we need to
worry about you?

481
00:31:09,067 --> 00:31:11,903
Not if I'm left alone
to do my job.

482
00:31:26,984 --> 00:31:28,219
<font color="#ffff00">WILLIAMS</font>:
From this moment forward

483
00:31:28,252 --> 00:31:30,989
you will not refer to the U-2
as a spy plane

484
00:31:31,021 --> 00:31:33,023
or reconnaissance aircraft.

485
00:31:33,057 --> 00:31:35,292
You will refer to it
as <i>"The Article."</i>

486
00:31:35,326 --> 00:31:38,462
<i>The Article</i>
has an 80-foot wingspan.

487
00:31:38,496 --> 00:31:44,001
It's powered by a single Pratt and Whitney
J57 P-37 engine.

488
00:31:44,035 --> 00:31:46,403
10,000 pounds of thrust

489
00:31:46,436 --> 00:31:50,474
and a maximum speed
of 430 miles an hour.

490
00:31:50,508 --> 00:31:52,242
She may not be quick,

491
00:31:52,277 --> 00:31:55,179
but at your cruising altitude
of 70,000 feet,

492
00:31:55,212 --> 00:31:57,015
nothing will
come close to you.

493
00:31:57,048 --> 00:31:58,182
<font color="#ffff00">POWERS</font>: 70,000?

494
00:31:58,215 --> 00:32:01,184
The idea is the enemy doesn't
even know you're there.

495
00:32:01,219 --> 00:32:03,086
It looks kind of flimsy.

496
00:32:06,089 --> 00:32:10,094
Every extra pound of weight
costs a foot in altitude,

497
00:32:10,127 --> 00:32:11,962
and we need
to accommodate the cameras.

498
00:32:14,097 --> 00:32:18,068
That's a 4,500-millimeter
lens, times three.

499
00:32:18,102 --> 00:32:20,404
A pin-sharp
panoramic camera

500
00:32:20,438 --> 00:32:25,076
four times as powerful as any aerial
reconnaissance cameras we've ever used.

501
00:32:25,108 --> 00:32:28,313
You're gonna be taking
pictures, lots of pictures.

502
00:32:28,346 --> 00:32:30,615
At 70,000 feet,
<i>The Article</i> can photograph

503
00:32:30,647 --> 00:32:34,585
2,000 square miles
of territory in one pass.

504
00:32:34,618 --> 00:32:36,354
Now, drivers,

505
00:32:36,386 --> 00:32:39,523
give Agent Sumner
your undivided attention.

506
00:32:39,557 --> 00:32:43,627
The items you will need
on your mission are over here.

507
00:32:43,661 --> 00:32:46,364
<font color="#ffff00">BLASCO</font>: B camera,
Revere 8 model 40 magazine,

508
00:32:46,396 --> 00:32:49,232
Keystone Capri K-25, silver.

509
00:32:49,267 --> 00:32:51,234
Hollow-handle shaving brush.

510
00:32:51,269 --> 00:32:54,404
Complete set of cipher tables
on edible silver foil.

511
00:32:54,439 --> 00:32:56,273
Specialist photographic
equipment.

512
00:32:58,476 --> 00:33:01,179
Yeah, we, uh...
We get the idea. Thank you.

513
00:33:13,357 --> 00:33:16,426
Uh, excuse me,
Agent, uh, Bosco. "Blasco."

514
00:33:16,461 --> 00:33:18,296
<font color="#ffff00">BLASCO</font>: Sir.

515
00:33:18,328 --> 00:33:23,334
There seems to be
a subsection to this list here.

516
00:33:23,367 --> 00:33:25,369
Well, most of this stuff
came from his studio,

517
00:33:25,403 --> 00:33:27,563
but the rest is from the
hotel room he was holed up in.

518
00:33:27,672 --> 00:33:29,207
Where we found him.

519
00:33:30,540 --> 00:33:32,643
The hotel room you searched?

520
00:33:32,676 --> 00:33:34,444
Well, yeah.

521
00:33:34,479 --> 00:33:36,547
Can I see the warrant?

522
00:33:36,580 --> 00:33:38,182
The what?

523
00:33:39,584 --> 00:33:42,153
Yeah, explain this to me.
There was a warrant.

524
00:33:42,185 --> 00:33:43,520
There was a warrant.

525
00:33:43,554 --> 00:33:45,623
A civil detention writ
for the arrest of an alien,

526
00:33:45,655 --> 00:33:47,525
but there was
no search warrant

527
00:33:47,557 --> 00:33:49,359
for suspicion
of criminal activity.

528
00:33:49,394 --> 00:33:50,627
So, the search
and the evidence

529
00:33:50,661 --> 00:33:52,396
that is
the fruit of that search,

530
00:33:52,430 --> 00:33:54,531
that's all tainted
and should not be admitted.

531
00:33:54,565 --> 00:33:57,300
What protections is he due,
Your Honor? The man is not...

532
00:33:57,335 --> 00:34:00,171
The man is what?
He's Rudolf Ivanovich Abel.

533
00:34:00,203 --> 00:34:02,105
He is a Soviet citizen.

534
00:34:02,140 --> 00:34:03,407
He's not an American.

535
00:34:03,441 --> 00:34:07,577
1886,
<i>Yick Wo v. Hopkins.</i>

536
00:34:07,612 --> 00:34:10,715
The court held that even aliens,
in that case Chinese immigrants,

537
00:34:10,748 --> 00:34:14,117
could not be held to answer for a crime
without due process of law,

538
00:34:14,152 --> 00:34:16,311
including any alien that entered
the country illegally.

539
00:34:16,586 --> 00:34:18,188
The Department of Justice

540
00:34:18,222 --> 00:34:21,458
has its first allegiance
to the United States.

541
00:34:21,492 --> 00:34:23,594
I don't see how an alien,
if he's here illegally,

542
00:34:23,628 --> 00:34:26,496
how he suffered
deprivation of rights.

543
00:34:26,530 --> 00:34:28,331
Rights as what?

544
00:34:28,365 --> 00:34:30,201
An American?

545
00:34:30,233 --> 00:34:33,371
Rights as what, Counselor?

546
00:34:33,403 --> 00:34:35,206
We're in a battle
for civilization.

547
00:34:35,239 --> 00:34:38,443
This Russian spy came here
to threaten our way of life.

548
00:34:38,476 --> 00:34:40,545
Now, I have a courtroom
of people waiting.

549
00:34:40,577 --> 00:34:42,579
Get out there,
sit beside the Russian.

550
00:34:42,612 --> 00:34:44,748
Let's get this
over and done with.

551
00:34:44,782 --> 00:34:47,217
And your motion is denied.

552
00:34:47,251 --> 00:34:49,686
<font color="#80FF00">(SOFT MURMURING)</font>

553
00:34:49,719 --> 00:34:52,155
He should take some
insurance out on himself.

554
00:34:52,657 --> 00:34:53,757
How did we do?

555
00:34:53,791 --> 00:34:55,226
In there?

556
00:34:56,561 --> 00:34:57,628
Uh...

557
00:34:57,661 --> 00:34:59,630
Not too good.

558
00:34:59,664 --> 00:35:01,666
Apparently, you're not
an American citizen.

559
00:35:01,699 --> 00:35:03,634
That's true.

560
00:35:03,668 --> 00:35:05,436
And according
to your boss,

561
00:35:05,469 --> 00:35:07,137
you're not
a Soviet citizen either.

562
00:35:08,405 --> 00:35:10,474
Well, the boss
isn't always right

563
00:35:12,275 --> 00:35:13,710
but he's always the boss.

564
00:35:18,715 --> 00:35:19,715
Do you never worry?

565
00:35:20,818 --> 00:35:22,686
Would it help?

566
00:35:22,719 --> 00:35:23,788
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR OPENS)</font>

567
00:35:23,820 --> 00:35:25,188
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>

568
00:35:29,827 --> 00:35:30,827
All rise.

569
00:35:33,697 --> 00:35:37,434
<font color="#ffff00">ALL</font>: "I pledge allegiance
to the Flag"

570
00:35:37,467 --> 00:35:40,538
"of the United States
of America,"

571
00:35:40,570 --> 00:35:44,541
"and to the Republic
for which it stands,"

572
00:35:44,574 --> 00:35:49,447
"one Nation under God,
indivisible,"

573
00:35:49,480 --> 00:35:51,748
"with liberty and justice
for all."

574
00:35:55,786 --> 00:35:57,588
<font color="#ffff00">MAN</font>: <font color="#80FF00">(ON PROJECTOR)</font> <i>First,</i>
<i>you have to know what happens</i>

575
00:35:57,621 --> 00:35:59,657
<i>when an atomic bomb explodes.</i>

576
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:05,496
<i>There is a bright flash,
brighter than the sun.</i>

577
00:36:05,530 --> 00:36:08,666
<i>It can smash in buildings,
break windows all over town.</i>

578
00:36:11,735 --> 00:36:14,739
<i>But if you duck and cover
like Bert,</i>

579
00:36:14,771 --> 00:36:16,706
<i>you will be much safer.</i>

580
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:18,842
<i>There are
two kinds of attack,</i>

581
00:36:18,876 --> 00:36:21,311
<i>with warning
and without any warning.</i>

582
00:36:22,512 --> 00:36:23,648
<font color="#80FF00">(WATER RUNNING)</font>

583
00:36:23,681 --> 00:36:24,681
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Mmm.

584
00:36:26,384 --> 00:36:27,617
What's going on in here?

585
00:36:27,652 --> 00:36:29,519
<font color="#ffff00">ROGER</font>: Okay, Dad,
this is really important.

586
00:36:29,554 --> 00:36:31,588
When the war begins,
the first thing they do

587
00:36:31,621 --> 00:36:34,324
is cut off all the water
and all the electricity.

588
00:36:34,358 --> 00:36:36,460
So the first thing we should do
is keep this filled,

589
00:36:36,494 --> 00:36:37,827
use the shower in your room,

590
00:36:37,862 --> 00:36:40,364
use the sink downstairs,
the garden hose outside...

591
00:36:40,398 --> 00:36:42,465
Those are good ideas.

592
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:44,467
But, Roger, I don't see this

593
00:36:44,501 --> 00:36:46,382
as being something
you ever have to worry about.

594
00:36:47,804 --> 00:36:49,072
Yeah, but when
you hear the sirens,

595
00:36:49,106 --> 00:36:52,309
there may not be enough time
to fill the tub and the sinks.

596
00:36:53,344 --> 00:36:54,778
Not gonna be any sirens...

597
00:36:56,414 --> 00:36:57,748
What is this?

598
00:36:57,782 --> 00:36:58,815
When the bomb comes,

599
00:36:58,849 --> 00:37:01,686
the Reds will aim
for the Empire State Building.

600
00:37:01,719 --> 00:37:04,521
But the bomb goes off
at 10,000 feet up.

601
00:37:04,554 --> 00:37:06,489
In <i>Life</i> magazine,
it's called an airburst,

602
00:37:06,523 --> 00:37:08,525
and if the bomb
is 50 megatons,

603
00:37:08,559 --> 00:37:11,728
the blast wave goes
out and out and out

604
00:37:11,762 --> 00:37:14,297
and melts everything
from here to here.

605
00:37:14,331 --> 00:37:15,565
Including where we live.
Here.

606
00:37:16,833 --> 00:37:18,501
Yeah, right there.

607
00:37:18,536 --> 00:37:19,637
But, you know, Roger,

608
00:37:19,670 --> 00:37:22,306
no one is dropping
atomic bombs on us.

609
00:37:22,340 --> 00:37:23,708
But the Russians want to.

610
00:37:23,740 --> 00:37:25,643
You know that Russian guy
you're defending?

611
00:37:25,675 --> 00:37:26,744
He's here
to get things ready

612
00:37:26,777 --> 00:37:28,579
for the guys
who drop the bombs.

613
00:37:28,612 --> 00:37:30,681
No, no, he's not...
Not here for that.

614
00:37:30,715 --> 00:37:32,315
He's not even Russian.

615
00:37:32,349 --> 00:37:34,385
He was born, we think,
in Northern England.

616
00:37:34,418 --> 00:37:35,657
Yeah, but he's
a spy for Russia.

617
00:37:36,920 --> 00:37:38,922
I don't even understand
what you're doing.

618
00:37:38,956 --> 00:37:41,391
You're not a Communist,
so why are you defending one?

619
00:37:41,425 --> 00:37:43,393
Well, because it's my job.

620
00:37:43,427 --> 00:37:45,463
It never used to be.

621
00:38:14,992 --> 00:38:17,427
<font color="#ffff00">CLERK</font>: <i>In the case of the
United States of America</i>

622
00:38:17,460 --> 00:38:19,664
versus Rudolf Abel,

623
00:38:19,697 --> 00:38:22,365
as to the first count
in the indictment,

624
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:24,467
how do you
find the defendant?

625
00:38:24,501 --> 00:38:26,737
Guilty or not guilty?

626
00:38:26,771 --> 00:38:27,771
Guilty.

627
00:38:29,407 --> 00:38:30,641
<font color="#ffff00">CLERK</font>:
As to the second count?

628
00:38:30,974 --> 00:38:32,876
Guilty.

629
00:38:32,909 --> 00:38:34,978
<font color="#ffff00">CLERK</font>:
As to the third count?

630
00:38:35,012 --> 00:38:36,246
Guilty.

631
00:38:36,280 --> 00:38:38,648
Your Honor, I make a motion
to set aside these guilty verdicts

632
00:38:38,682 --> 00:38:40,418
against the weight
of evidence.

633
00:38:40,451 --> 00:38:41,952
Denied.

634
00:38:41,985 --> 00:38:43,853
The jury is discharged.

635
00:38:43,887 --> 00:38:45,922
Thank you all.

637
00:38:45,951 --> 00:38:49,793
I would like to add that,
if I were one of you,
I would've reached
the same verdict.

638
00:38:51,494 --> 00:38:57,963
Sentencing will be November 15.

640
00:39:05,943 --> 00:39:08,512
The death sentence is not
a foregone conclusion.

641
00:39:08,545 --> 00:39:09,547
Don't worry.

642
00:39:10,681 --> 00:39:12,849
I'm not afraid to die,
Mr. Donovan.

643
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:17,722
Although

644
00:39:17,755 --> 00:39:19,657
it wouldn't be
my first choice.

645
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:25,996
You left this behind.

646
00:39:27,664 --> 00:39:28,666
Oh, thank you.

647
00:39:31,534 --> 00:39:32,902
Oh, thank you very much!

648
00:39:53,056 --> 00:39:55,693
You've never asked me
if the charges were true.

649
00:39:58,496 --> 00:40:01,431
If I am indeed a spy.

650
00:40:03,567 --> 00:40:05,068
This is how we do it.

651
00:40:05,101 --> 00:40:07,471
The case against you matters.

652
00:40:07,505 --> 00:40:08,873
Making them prove it matters.

653
00:40:08,905 --> 00:40:10,807
The fiction is...

654
00:40:10,840 --> 00:40:12,809
Whether you did it or not
doesn't matter.

655
00:40:12,842 --> 00:40:15,813
The state has to prove it,
that you're a spy.

656
00:40:15,846 --> 00:40:17,547
So you're not curious?

657
00:40:17,981 --> 00:40:19,550
No, not really.

658
00:40:19,583 --> 00:40:22,085
I always assumed
you were an artist. <font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

659
00:40:22,119 --> 00:40:24,054
My wife, she's the artist.

660
00:40:24,088 --> 00:40:26,690
She's a musician

661
00:40:26,724 --> 00:40:30,527
in the Children's Orchestra
of Moscow.

662
00:40:30,561 --> 00:40:31,896
What instrument?

663
00:40:31,929 --> 00:40:33,797
The harp.

664
00:40:33,831 --> 00:40:34,831
Oh.

665
00:40:35,865 --> 00:40:37,568
Then, she's an angel.

666
00:40:47,878 --> 00:40:50,614
Standing there like that,
you remind me of a man

667
00:40:50,648 --> 00:40:52,882
who used to come
to our house when I was young.

668
00:40:54,150 --> 00:40:57,655
My father used to say,
"Watch this man."

669
00:40:58,822 --> 00:41:02,092
So I did,
every time he came.

670
00:41:02,126 --> 00:41:05,094
And never once

671
00:41:05,128 --> 00:41:07,965
did he do
anything remarkable.

672
00:41:10,166 --> 00:41:12,536
And I remind you of him?

673
00:41:13,771 --> 00:41:15,840
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)</font>

674
00:41:15,873 --> 00:41:19,777
This one time, I was
about the age of your son.

675
00:41:19,809 --> 00:41:22,913
Our house was overrun
by partisan border guards.

676
00:41:26,717 --> 00:41:29,018
Dozens of them.

677
00:41:29,052 --> 00:41:31,088
My father was beaten,
my mother was beaten,

678
00:41:31,121 --> 00:41:34,525
and this man,
my father's friend, he was beaten.

679
00:41:36,193 --> 00:41:38,128
And I watched this man.

680
00:41:41,632 --> 00:41:43,000
Every time they hit him

681
00:41:45,135 --> 00:41:46,836
he stood back up again.

682
00:41:49,039 --> 00:41:50,608
So they hit him harder.

683
00:41:52,876 --> 00:41:54,945
Still,
he got back to his feet.

684
00:41:58,648 --> 00:42:00,885
I think because of this
they stopped the beating.

685
00:42:00,918 --> 00:42:02,619
They let him live.

686
00:42:05,688 --> 00:42:07,157
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING RUSSIAN)</font>

687
00:42:09,760 --> 00:42:10,793
<font color="#80FF00">(LAUGHING)</font>

688
00:42:10,827 --> 00:42:12,862
I remember them saying it.

689
00:42:14,697 --> 00:42:15,900
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING RUSSIAN)</font>

690
00:42:20,704 --> 00:42:22,773
It sort of means, like, uh,

691
00:42:25,108 --> 00:42:26,577
"standing man."

692
00:42:30,213 --> 00:42:31,213
Standing man.

693
00:42:37,153 --> 00:42:38,956
<font color="#80FF00">(DOORBELL RINGS)</font>

694
00:42:43,092 --> 00:42:45,695
Hello, Jim!
Millie, you're a vision.

695
00:42:45,728 --> 00:42:48,097
Mortie's busy
getting kitted out.

696
00:42:48,132 --> 00:42:49,632
But come on in.

697
00:42:51,135 --> 00:42:53,804
Scotch, Jim?
Oh, yes, please. Thank you.

698
00:42:53,836 --> 00:42:55,672
Uh, just a drop of water.

699
00:42:56,673 --> 00:42:57,942
Your Honor.

700
00:42:57,974 --> 00:43:00,844
Hello, Jim!
Just going out.

701
00:43:00,878 --> 00:43:02,846
Got a few minutes though.

702
00:43:02,880 --> 00:43:05,849
March of Dimes thing.
Millie is active.

703
00:43:05,882 --> 00:43:07,251
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

704
00:43:07,284 --> 00:43:09,052
Well, thank you
for seeing me.

705
00:43:09,085 --> 00:43:13,123
I just wanted to give you
my two cents on the sentencing.

706
00:43:13,157 --> 00:43:16,260
And I thought maybe
I should pester you at home

707
00:43:16,293 --> 00:43:19,262
as not all of my points
are narrowly legal.

708
00:43:19,295 --> 00:43:22,065
Yeah, well,
it's that kind of case.

709
00:43:22,099 --> 00:43:24,134
I hope I wasn't too scratchy
during the trial

710
00:43:24,168 --> 00:43:27,003
but it's exactly
what you say.

711
00:43:27,036 --> 00:43:28,972
Nothing about this
is narrowly legal.

712
00:43:30,773 --> 00:43:31,909
There are bigger issues.

713
00:43:33,309 --> 00:43:34,978
Bigger issues!

714
00:43:37,313 --> 00:43:39,083
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>
Thank you, thank you.

715
00:43:39,115 --> 00:43:40,117
Uh...

716
00:43:42,119 --> 00:43:43,887
Sir...
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>

717
00:43:43,920 --> 00:43:45,922
I think it could be
considered

718
00:43:45,956 --> 00:43:48,726
in the best interests
of the United States

719
00:43:48,759 --> 00:43:51,695
that Abel remain alive.

720
00:43:52,195 --> 00:43:53,998
Why?

721
00:43:54,030 --> 00:43:55,766
I'm not saying
I've made up my mind

722
00:43:55,798 --> 00:43:56,934
but if he was
gonna cooperate,

723
00:43:56,967 --> 00:43:59,135
work with the government,
he would've done it already.

724
00:43:59,168 --> 00:44:00,269
- True, but the issue here...
- You can't...

725
00:44:00,304 --> 00:44:02,005
Excuse me.

726
00:44:06,809 --> 00:44:09,913
You can't say it's in the best interest
of the United States

727
00:44:09,947 --> 00:44:13,916
that he spend
the rest of his days in a prison cell.

728
00:44:13,951 --> 00:44:16,019
How is this
the national interest?

729
00:44:16,052 --> 00:44:18,188
Not the incarceration
itself, sir.

730
00:44:19,856 --> 00:44:22,925
It is possible that,
in the foreseeable future,

731
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:25,195
an American
of equivalent rank

732
00:44:25,228 --> 00:44:27,965
might be captured
by Soviet Russia.

733
00:44:27,998 --> 00:44:30,233
We might want to have
someone to trade.

734
00:44:30,266 --> 00:44:31,934
Wow.

735
00:44:31,969 --> 00:44:34,137
That sounds like
spinning what-ifs.

736
00:44:34,170 --> 00:44:36,072
You could do that
till the cows come home.

737
00:44:36,106 --> 00:44:39,043
It's my business, what-ifs.
I'm in insurance.

738
00:44:39,076 --> 00:44:41,344
And there's nothing
implausible about this one.

739
00:44:41,378 --> 00:44:44,782
It's entirely in the realm
of what could happen.

740
00:44:44,815 --> 00:44:48,184
It's the kind of probability that
people buy insurance for.

741
00:44:48,217 --> 00:44:52,155
If we send this guy to his death,
we leave ourselves wide open.

742
00:44:52,188 --> 00:44:54,958
No policy
in our back pocket

743
00:44:54,992 --> 00:44:56,793
for the day the storm comes.

744
00:44:58,996 --> 00:45:01,231
Nice speech.

745
00:45:01,264 --> 00:45:06,903
Sir, there is also
the humanitarian argument.

746
00:45:06,936 --> 00:45:10,007
Should he die for doing the job
they sent him to do?

747
00:45:12,141 --> 00:45:13,277
All right, Counselor.

748
00:45:16,012 --> 00:45:17,047
I gotta run.

749
00:45:20,983 --> 00:45:22,152
Good seeing you, Jim.

750
00:45:26,356 --> 00:45:27,824
I'll walk you out.

751
00:45:29,126 --> 00:45:30,827
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)</font>

752
00:45:31,295 --> 00:45:32,295
<font color="#ffff00">BAILIFF</font>: All rise.

753
00:45:34,030 --> 00:45:35,331
The United States
District Court

754
00:45:35,364 --> 00:45:38,034
for the Eastern District
of New York is now in session.

755
00:45:38,068 --> 00:45:40,137
The honorable
Mortimer Byers presiding.

756
00:45:44,842 --> 00:45:46,175
Be seated.

757
00:45:58,088 --> 00:45:59,889
BYERS: The defendant
will please rise.

758
00:46:14,438 --> 00:46:16,172
In the measured judgment
of this court

759
00:46:16,206 --> 00:46:17,440
the following sentence,

760
00:46:17,474 --> 00:46:19,976
based upon the jury's
verdict of guilty

761
00:46:20,010 --> 00:46:22,311
as to each count
of the indictment,

762
00:46:22,346 --> 00:46:27,150
is believed to meet the test
which has been stated.

763
00:46:27,184 --> 00:46:30,086
Pursuant to the verdict
of guilty as to all counts,

764
00:46:32,422 --> 00:46:34,324
the defendant is committed
to the custody

765
00:46:34,358 --> 00:46:36,425
of the Attorney General
of the United States

766
00:46:38,461 --> 00:46:40,963
for imprisonment
in a federal institution

767
00:46:40,998 --> 00:46:44,434
to be selected by him
for a period of 30 years.

768
00:46:44,467 --> 00:46:45,536
<font color="#80FF00">(GAVEL BANGS)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(CROWD MURMURING)</font>

769
00:46:45,568 --> 00:46:47,938
Marshals, you may take
the defendant into custody.

770
00:46:47,971 --> 00:46:49,072
No... No electrocution?

771
00:46:49,106 --> 00:46:50,474
No. No, no.

772
00:46:50,507 --> 00:46:52,242
Why aren't we hanging him?

773
00:46:52,275 --> 00:46:53,476
In the name of God,
why aren't we hanging him?

774
00:46:53,510 --> 00:46:55,411
Sit down, sir!

775
00:46:55,445 --> 00:46:59,148
<font color="#ffff00">MAN 1</font>: He's a spy!
He's killing us with his lies!

776
00:46:59,182 --> 00:47:00,918
In the name of God,
why aren't we hanging him?

777
00:47:00,951 --> 00:47:01,952
That's right!

778
00:47:01,985 --> 00:47:03,487
<font color="#80FF00">(CLAMORING)</font>

779
00:47:03,519 --> 00:47:05,255
<font color="#ffff00">MAN 2</font>: He's a spy!

780
00:47:08,358 --> 00:47:09,425
<font color="#80FF00">(CAMERAS FLASHING)</font>

781
00:47:09,458 --> 00:47:11,360
<font color="#ffff00">REPORTER 1</font>:
Mr. Donovan! Mr. Donovan!

782
00:47:11,394 --> 00:47:14,364
Just one question.
Just one question, sir.

783
00:47:14,398 --> 00:47:16,266
<font color="#80FF00">(REPORTERS CLAMORING)</font>

784
00:47:21,405 --> 00:47:23,172
My hat! My hat! My hat!

785
00:47:25,275 --> 00:47:26,943
Nobody here has a comment.

786
00:47:26,976 --> 00:47:28,512
Mr. and Mrs. Donovan
are not answering questions.

787
00:47:28,545 --> 00:47:29,947
<font color="#ffff00">REPORTER 2</font>:
How do you feel now that

788
00:47:29,980 --> 00:47:31,148
this national nightmare
is over?

789
00:47:31,181 --> 00:47:32,481
<font color="#ffff00">REPORTER 3</font>: Mr. Donovan, are
you happy with the verdict?

790
00:47:32,516 --> 00:47:36,119
We're... We're in
firm ground on, uh, for a case on appeal.

791
00:47:36,152 --> 00:47:37,393
<font color="#ffff00">REPORTER 4</font>: Appeal?
Appeal, yes.

792
00:47:51,568 --> 00:47:53,402
<font color="#ffff00">TOM</font>: Jim, you did a great job.

793
00:47:53,436 --> 00:47:56,206
You fulfilled your mandate
and then some.

794
00:47:56,239 --> 00:47:58,242
But the man is a spy.

795
00:47:58,275 --> 00:48:00,978
The verdict is correct
and there's no reason to appeal it.

796
00:48:01,010 --> 00:48:02,945
There's ample
procedural reason.

797
00:48:02,980 --> 00:48:04,047
We know the search
is tainted.

798
00:48:04,081 --> 00:48:05,248
And Fourth Amendment issues

799
00:48:05,282 --> 00:48:07,284
will always weigh more heavily
in an appellate forum.

800
00:48:07,317 --> 00:48:09,018
We've got a good shot.

801
00:48:09,052 --> 00:48:12,021
What the goddamn hell
are you talking about?

802
00:48:12,054 --> 00:48:15,259
We were supposed to show
he had a capable defense, which we did.

803
00:48:15,291 --> 00:48:17,561
Why are you citing
the goddamn Constitution at me?

804
00:48:17,594 --> 00:48:19,295
Tom, if you
look me in the eye

805
00:48:19,329 --> 00:48:21,364
and tell me
we don't have grounds

806
00:48:21,398 --> 00:48:22,398
for an appeal,
I'll drop it right now.

807
00:48:22,431 --> 00:48:24,568
I'm not saying that.
You know what I'm saying.

808
00:48:24,601 --> 00:48:27,036
Tom is saying there's
a cost to these things.

809
00:48:27,070 --> 00:48:28,572
That's right.

810
00:48:28,605 --> 00:48:31,240
A cost to both
your family and your firm.

811
00:48:37,213 --> 00:48:39,081
<font color="#80FF00">(SOFT ORCHESTRAL
MUSIC PLAYING)</font>

812
00:49:06,242 --> 00:49:09,079
I've missed music.

813
00:49:09,112 --> 00:49:11,447
They checked the radio to make
sure there was no transmitter in it.

814
00:49:11,481 --> 00:49:13,349
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>
I'm sure they did.

815
00:49:15,518 --> 00:49:17,387
Shostakovich. Mmm.

816
00:49:19,489 --> 00:49:22,092
That's a very great artist,
Shostakovich.

817
00:49:37,574 --> 00:49:39,141
Uh, I think

818
00:49:40,543 --> 00:49:44,514
our strongest grounds
for reversal

819
00:49:44,547 --> 00:49:45,547
are constitutional.

820
00:49:48,684 --> 00:49:52,422
Our best chance is if the Supreme Court
agrees to review our case.

821
00:49:56,393 --> 00:49:58,061
<font color="#80FF00">(MUSIC STOPS)</font>

822
00:49:58,094 --> 00:49:59,295
Jim...

823
00:50:06,202 --> 00:50:07,336
You should be careful.

824
00:50:13,175 --> 00:50:14,311
Careful.

825
00:50:16,579 --> 00:50:19,215
<i>♪ 77 Sunset Strip</i>

826
00:50:19,248 --> 00:50:21,351
<font color="#ffff00">TV ANNOUNCER</font>:
<i>Edward Byrnes.</i>

827
00:50:21,385 --> 00:50:24,253
<i>♪ 77 Sunset Strip</i>

828
00:50:24,286 --> 00:50:25,487
<font color="#ffff00">TV ANNOUNCER</font>:
<i>And Richard Long.</i>

829
00:50:26,722 --> 00:50:28,324
<font color="#ffff00">DICK</font>:
<i>Good morning, Suzanne.</i>

830
00:50:28,358 --> 00:50:29,659
<font color="#ffff00">SUZANNE</font>:
<i>Good morning, Dick.</i>

831
00:50:29,693 --> 00:50:31,572
<i>There is a special
delivery letter here for you.</i>

832
00:50:32,596 --> 00:50:33,663
<font color="#ffff00">DICK</font>:
<i>Oh, her.</i>

833
00:50:34,597 --> 00:50:35,597
<font color="#80FF00">(GUNSHOT)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPS)</font>

834
00:50:38,201 --> 00:50:39,436
<font color="#80FF00">(GUNSHOTS)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPING)</font>

835
00:50:44,206 --> 00:50:45,206
Mom!

836
00:50:47,210 --> 00:50:49,278
What in God's name happened?

837
00:50:49,311 --> 00:50:50,713
Honey, are you all right?

838
00:50:50,746 --> 00:50:52,282
Mom!
Are you hurt?

839
00:50:52,315 --> 00:50:53,434
<font color="#ffff00">MARY</font>: I've got her.
Upstairs!

840
00:50:56,519 --> 00:50:58,320
Are you all right?
I sat with my back against the wall, Dad!

841
00:50:58,355 --> 00:51:00,157
Are you okay?
Are you all right?

842
00:51:00,190 --> 00:51:01,557
Dad, I sat with
my back against the wall!

843
00:51:01,590 --> 00:51:02,659
Good, good.
Away from the windows!

844
00:51:02,692 --> 00:51:04,260
Good, good,
good, good, good.

845
00:51:04,293 --> 00:51:06,496
Good boy,
good boy, good boy.

846
00:51:06,530 --> 00:51:08,197
It's okay.
It's all right. <font color="#80FF00">(SOBBING)</font>

847
00:51:08,231 --> 00:51:10,367
It's all right,
they're gone, they're gone.

848
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:12,235
They're cowards.
They're gone.

849
00:51:12,268 --> 00:51:14,637
We have detectives
canvassing the neighborhood.

850
00:51:14,670 --> 00:51:16,572
Maybe we'll find
a description of the car

851
00:51:16,606 --> 00:51:18,375
but I don't think
it's very likely.

852
00:51:18,407 --> 00:51:20,309
We're gonna
need protection.

853
00:51:20,342 --> 00:51:22,711
Oh, I have instructions
to leave two cars here.

854
00:51:22,746 --> 00:51:23,612
It'll be pretty obvious

855
00:51:23,646 --> 00:51:24,648
that this is a secure...
For how long?

856
00:51:24,681 --> 00:51:27,184
I have to be in Washington
later on this week.

857
00:51:27,217 --> 00:51:28,585
What are you doing?

858
00:51:28,617 --> 00:51:29,753
What are you doing
to your family?

859
00:51:29,786 --> 00:51:31,454
Pardon me, Officer?

860
00:51:31,487 --> 00:51:32,621
You're still
defending this guy?

861
00:51:32,655 --> 00:51:33,389
I mean, what are you
fighting for him for?

862
00:51:33,422 --> 00:51:35,492
Okay, knock that off,
Officer.

863
00:51:35,525 --> 00:51:36,760
I'll knock off nothing.

864
00:51:36,793 --> 00:51:38,427
I was in the third wave
at Omaha Beach.

865
00:51:38,461 --> 00:51:39,563
Hey, I did my time
in the service.

866
00:51:39,596 --> 00:51:41,498
I was fighting these guys,
and you're defending them?

867
00:51:41,530 --> 00:51:44,233
Why don't you just
do your job as an officer of the law?

868
00:51:44,266 --> 00:51:45,768
Okay, I'm sorry.
Let's go outside. Come on.

869
00:51:45,802 --> 00:51:47,336
You know what?
Just forget him.

870
00:51:47,369 --> 00:51:48,637
What am I not getting here?

871
00:51:48,672 --> 00:51:52,641
Outside, Officer. I'm sorry.

872
00:51:52,675 --> 00:51:55,478
Defending a commie spy. Jeez!
<font color="#80FF00">(REPORTERS CLAMORING)</font>

873
00:52:12,795 --> 00:52:15,331
<font color="#ffff00">REPORTER</font>: Do you feel
that you deserve this, sir?

874
00:52:31,313 --> 00:52:32,782
<font color="#80FF00">(CLAMORING CONTINUES)</font>

875
00:52:37,586 --> 00:52:38,755
<font color="#ffff00">WILLIAMS</font>:
Should it become necessary

876
00:52:38,788 --> 00:52:41,824
to abandon the aircraft
over Soviet territory,

877
00:52:41,858 --> 00:52:44,427
then there's a two-and-a-half pound
explosive charge

878
00:52:44,460 --> 00:52:46,695
contained within
the fuselage.

879
00:52:46,730 --> 00:52:49,699
Now, first,
you activate the circuits.

880
00:52:51,768 --> 00:52:54,637
But to start
the 70-second timer,

881
00:52:54,670 --> 00:52:57,574
you need to flip the switch
marked "Destruct."

882
00:53:00,543 --> 00:53:04,681
It is imperative that these
flights remain a secret

883
00:53:04,713 --> 00:53:08,251
and this equipment
does not fall into enemy hands.

884
00:53:08,284 --> 00:53:10,420
What about us?

885
00:53:10,452 --> 00:53:12,621
I don't know if you're kidding,
Lieutenant Powers.

886
00:53:12,655 --> 00:53:14,324
I'm not.

887
00:53:16,559 --> 00:53:17,860
What you know
about the plane

888
00:53:17,893 --> 00:53:20,262
is as secret
as the plane itself.

889
00:53:22,431 --> 00:53:25,601
If capture
is a foregone conclusion,

890
00:53:25,635 --> 00:53:27,670
you go down with your plane.

891
00:53:27,704 --> 00:53:30,606
Now, if you think
you can ditch and get away,

892
00:53:30,639 --> 00:53:32,309
if you're close enough
to a border, fine.

893
00:53:32,342 --> 00:53:33,577
You know
the ejection protocol.

894
00:53:36,346 --> 00:53:38,748
But if you ditch,

895
00:53:38,782 --> 00:53:40,884
you bring the dollar with you.

896
00:53:40,916 --> 00:53:43,452
All right,
there's a pin inside.

897
00:53:45,688 --> 00:53:48,625
You scratch your skin
anywhere, it's instantaneous.

898
00:53:48,657 --> 00:53:51,628
If you think you are
about to be captured,

899
00:53:51,661 --> 00:53:52,829
you use it.

900
00:53:56,800 --> 00:53:58,735
Drivers, you understand me?

901
00:54:00,603 --> 00:54:02,271
Spend the dollar.

902
00:54:15,885 --> 00:54:17,286
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

903
00:54:56,960 --> 00:54:59,528
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Mr. Chief Justice,
and may it please the court.

904
00:54:59,561 --> 00:55:02,898
The "Cold War" is not
just a phrase, Your Honor.

905
00:55:02,931 --> 00:55:05,568
It's not just
a figure of speech.

906
00:55:05,601 --> 00:55:07,737
Truly,
a battle is being fought

907
00:55:07,771 --> 00:55:11,574
between two competing views
of the world.

908
00:55:11,608 --> 00:55:14,844
I contend
that Rudolf Ivanovich Abel,

909
00:55:14,878 --> 00:55:16,980
"Colonel Abel,"
as he was called

910
00:55:17,012 --> 00:55:19,449
even by the men
who arrested him,

911
00:55:19,481 --> 00:55:22,619
is our foe in that battle.

912
00:55:22,652 --> 00:55:24,387
Auxiliary power?

913
00:55:24,420 --> 00:55:25,989
Connected and on.

914
00:55:26,021 --> 00:55:28,458
Oxygen seat-pack supply?

915
00:55:28,490 --> 00:55:29,925
Supply at 1,800 psi.

916
00:55:29,958 --> 00:55:31,628
Inverter?

917
00:55:31,661 --> 00:55:32,795
On, light out.

918
00:55:32,829 --> 00:55:35,998
He was treated
as a combatant in that war

919
00:55:36,032 --> 00:55:40,003
until it no longer
suited our government to so treat him.

920
00:55:40,036 --> 00:55:42,405
Accordingly,

921
00:55:42,438 --> 00:55:45,875
he was not given the protections
we give our own citizens.

922
00:55:45,909 --> 00:55:47,643
He was subjected
to treatment that,

923
00:55:47,677 --> 00:55:50,447
however appropriate
for a suspected enemy,

924
00:55:50,480 --> 00:55:52,949
was not appropriate
to a suspected criminal.

925
00:55:52,981 --> 00:55:54,416
Fuel transfer?

926
00:55:54,451 --> 00:55:55,518
Transfer switch off.

927
00:55:55,552 --> 00:55:56,652
Destructor?

928
00:55:56,686 --> 00:55:57,820
Destructor safety.

929
00:55:57,853 --> 00:55:59,021
Climb data card.

930
00:55:59,054 --> 00:56:00,590
Installed in aircraft.

931
00:56:14,737 --> 00:56:16,739
I know this man.

932
00:56:16,773 --> 00:56:20,577
If the charge is true, he serves a
foreign power but he serves it faithfully.

933
00:56:20,610 --> 00:56:24,547
If he is a soldier in the opposing army,
he is a good soldier.

934
00:56:24,581 --> 00:56:27,516
He has not fled the field
of battle to save himself.

935
00:56:27,550 --> 00:56:29,719
He has refused
to serve his captor.

936
00:56:29,753 --> 00:56:32,054
He has refused
to betray his cause.

937
00:56:32,088 --> 00:56:34,657
He has refused
to take the coward's way out.

938
00:56:34,690 --> 00:56:37,527
The coward
must abandon his dignity

939
00:56:37,559 --> 00:56:39,061
before he abandons
the field of battle.

940
00:56:39,094 --> 00:56:42,532
That,
Rudolf Abel will never do.

941
00:56:42,565 --> 00:56:43,666
Shouldn't we,

942
00:56:43,699 --> 00:56:45,869
by giving him
the full benefit

943
00:56:45,902 --> 00:56:48,505
of the rights that define
our system of government,

944
00:56:48,538 --> 00:56:50,940
show this man who we are?

945
00:56:50,974 --> 00:56:53,777
Who we are.

946
00:56:53,809 --> 00:56:58,081
Is that not the greatest weapon
we have in this Cold War?

947
00:56:58,114 --> 00:57:01,083
Will we stand by our cause
less resolutely

948
00:57:01,117 --> 00:57:03,052
than he stands by his?

949
00:57:36,985 --> 00:57:38,588
<font color="#80FF00">(MISSILES WHIZZING)</font>

950
00:57:40,657 --> 00:57:41,657
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPING)</font>

951
00:57:57,639 --> 00:57:58,875
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPING)</font>

952
00:58:28,737 --> 00:58:30,072
<font color="#80FF00">(SCREAMING)</font>

953
00:58:38,181 --> 00:58:39,181
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPING)</font>

954
00:58:39,215 --> 00:58:40,782
<font color="#80FF00">(GRUNTING)</font>

955
00:58:57,766 --> 00:58:58,902
<font color="#80FF00">(BEEPING)</font>

956
00:59:07,844 --> 00:59:08,911
<font color="#80FF00">(SCREAMS)</font>

957
00:59:16,184 --> 00:59:17,186
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPING)</font>

958
00:59:34,269 --> 00:59:35,938
<font color="#80FF00">(PANTING)</font>

959
00:59:47,083 --> 00:59:48,818
<font color="#ffff00">RADIO ANNOUNCER</font>:
<i>The President announced</i>

960
00:59:48,851 --> 00:59:52,789
<i>that a NASA weather plane disappeared
four days ago north of Turkey.</i>

961
00:59:52,822 --> 00:59:54,724
<i>And the Space
Administration fears</i>

962
00:59:54,757 --> 00:59:56,992
<i>that its pilot died
in the crash.</i>

963
00:59:57,025 --> 00:59:59,862
<i>Radar showed the plane
wandering off course</i>

964
00:59:59,896 --> 01:00:03,865
<i>deep into Soviet territory
before contact was lost,</i>

965
01:00:03,900 --> 01:00:05,934
<i>complicating any effort
to recover it</i>

966
01:00:05,969 --> 01:00:08,003
<i>or the remains of the pilot.</i>

967
01:00:08,036 --> 01:00:10,273
<i>Now to news
out of Washington.</i>

968
01:00:10,306 --> 01:00:11,373
<i>In a close vote today,</i>

969
01:00:11,407 --> 01:00:14,077
<i>the Supreme Court
affirmed the conviction</i>

970
01:00:14,110 --> 01:00:16,913
<i>of Russian spy
Rudolf Ivanovich Abel,</i>

971
01:00:16,945 --> 01:00:18,880
<i>five votes to four.</i>

972
01:00:18,914 --> 01:00:21,150
<i>The case presented
civil liberties issues,</i>

973
01:00:21,184 --> 01:00:24,954
<i>according to the Russian's lawyer,
James B. Donovan.</i>

975
01:00:24,980 --> 01:00:31,059
<i>When asked how he felt
about today's loss,</i>
<i>Mr. Donovan simply said,
"Tired."</i>

976
01:00:31,094 --> 01:00:34,230
<i>I'll have more news for you
after this message.</i>

977
01:00:34,262 --> 01:00:35,898
<font color="#ffff00">WOMAN</font>: <font color="#80FF00">(SINGING)</font>
<i>♪ Styling tricks</i>

978
01:00:35,931 --> 01:00:36,965
<font color="#80FF00">(WHISPERS)</font> It's over.

979
01:00:37,000 --> 01:00:38,101
<i>♪ See the new,
exciting styling tricks</i>

980
01:00:38,134 --> 01:00:39,702
<i>♪ You...</i>

981
01:00:39,735 --> 01:00:41,137
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

982
01:00:42,170 --> 01:00:44,106
Marty.

983
01:00:44,139 --> 01:00:47,010
I'll be damned if I can find the file
on that Prudential case.

984
01:00:47,043 --> 01:00:50,313
I, uh, Mr. Watters had me
move it over to Jack Elwes's office

985
01:00:50,346 --> 01:00:52,248
along with the first draft
of the appeal.

986
01:00:52,280 --> 01:00:53,782
He did?

987
01:00:53,815 --> 01:00:56,985
Yeah, he redrafted and filed that last week
while you were in Washington.

988
01:01:00,023 --> 01:01:00,922
<font color="#ffff00">TOM</font>: How long
have we been together?

989
01:01:00,956 --> 01:01:03,793
You act as if
I've never closed a deal.

990
01:01:03,826 --> 01:01:06,829
Set up a meeting.
Someplace fancy.

991
01:01:06,862 --> 01:01:09,032
I'm betting Mr. Young
would like some of the finer things.

992
01:01:09,065 --> 01:01:10,800
New York has to offer.

993
01:01:10,833 --> 01:01:12,802
And I want you to give me
the summary of...

994
01:01:31,086 --> 01:01:33,121
Do you know anyone
from Leipzig?

995
01:01:33,155 --> 01:01:37,827
"Leipzig." Am I getting
hate mail from Germany now?

996
01:01:37,860 --> 01:01:38,994
From East Germany.

997
01:01:45,101 --> 01:01:46,168
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

998
01:01:48,036 --> 01:01:50,005
Is it your wife?

999
01:01:50,039 --> 01:01:51,039
No.

1000
01:01:52,108 --> 01:01:54,309
No, no, no, no.

1001
01:01:54,342 --> 01:01:58,047
She pretends to be
but it's not even a good pretense.

1002
01:01:59,282 --> 01:02:02,018
"I'm taking this liberty
to write to you"

1003
01:02:02,050 --> 01:02:04,286
"after having learned
from the newspapers"

1004
01:02:04,320 --> 01:02:07,123
"about your most
humane attitude"

1005
01:02:07,155 --> 01:02:11,126
"towards my
beloved husband, Rudolf Abel."

1006
01:02:11,159 --> 01:02:12,260
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1007
01:02:12,295 --> 01:02:14,963
And do I write back?

1008
01:02:17,833 --> 01:02:19,101
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1009
01:02:22,871 --> 01:02:26,242
What's the next move
when you don't know what the game is?

1010
01:02:28,210 --> 01:02:30,112
<font color="#ffff00">TRANSLATOR</font>:
<i>Francis Gary Powers...</i>

1011
01:02:30,146 --> 01:02:31,414
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING RUSSIAN)</font>

1012
01:02:31,447 --> 01:02:36,818
On the strength of Article Two
of the U.S.S.R. law

1013
01:02:36,853 --> 01:02:40,422
on criminal responsibility
for state crimes

1014
01:02:40,456 --> 01:02:42,958
to 10 years of confinement

1015
01:02:42,992 --> 01:02:45,360
with the first three years
to be served in prison.

1016
01:02:47,963 --> 01:02:49,431
The term of the punishment,

1017
01:02:49,465 --> 01:02:52,335
including
preliminary detention,

1018
01:02:52,367 --> 01:02:55,036
shall be counted
from May first,

1019
01:02:55,070 --> 01:02:57,206
nineteen hundred and sixty.

1020
01:02:57,239 --> 01:02:59,275
<font color="#80FF00">(CONTINUES SPEAKING
IN RUSSIAN)</font>

1021
01:03:02,311 --> 01:03:04,146
<font color="#80FF00">(CAMERA SHUTTERS CLICKING)</font>

1022
01:03:23,032 --> 01:03:25,367
<font color="#ffff00">ALLEN</font>: <i>Mr. Donovan,
thank you so much for coming.</i>

1023
01:03:25,400 --> 01:03:27,869
It's a pleasure to meet you,
Mr. Dulles.

1024
01:03:27,903 --> 01:03:29,070
It's an honor, sir.

1025
01:03:30,373 --> 01:03:34,476
Nescafe, cream, two lumps.

1026
01:03:34,510 --> 01:03:36,978
Thank you.

1027
01:03:37,012 --> 01:03:39,181
Okay,
this letter you received.

1028
01:03:39,215 --> 01:03:41,918
Seems pretty clear that the Soviets
are making an overture here.

1029
01:03:43,119 --> 01:03:44,586
They're doing it
through East Germany

1030
01:03:44,619 --> 01:03:46,989
because they still don't want
to acknowledge Abel as a Soviet citizen,

1031
01:03:47,023 --> 01:03:49,157
much less a Soviet spy. So...

1032
01:03:50,492 --> 01:03:52,195
Lot of fiction going on.

1033
01:03:52,228 --> 01:03:54,463
Yes, sir, but to what end?

1034
01:03:54,496 --> 01:03:56,932
They got our guy,
our spy pilot.

1035
01:03:56,965 --> 01:03:58,166
We've got their guy.

1036
01:04:01,137 --> 01:04:04,407
Prisoner exchange,
I think that's what they're after.

1037
01:04:04,440 --> 01:04:07,076
Which could hardly be
a surprise to you, Counselor.

1038
01:04:07,108 --> 01:04:10,045
It's an eventuality
that I think you foresaw.

1039
01:04:10,079 --> 01:04:13,248
Yes, I sure did,
and I'm so rarely right. <font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1040
01:04:13,282 --> 01:04:16,385
Well, congratulations.
Red letter day.

1041
01:04:16,418 --> 01:04:20,489
Now, we have our man
over there. Powers.

1042
01:04:20,523 --> 01:04:24,460
Good man, but with
a head full of classified information.

1043
01:04:24,492 --> 01:04:27,128
Abel has a head
full of classified information, too,

1044
01:04:27,163 --> 01:04:29,130
but he hasn't
given us a lick.

1045
01:04:29,164 --> 01:04:30,967
And, sir, take it from me,
he won't.

1046
01:04:31,000 --> 01:04:33,369
Yes, we know that,
but the Russians don't.

1047
01:04:33,402 --> 01:04:36,271
They want their man back
before he cracks.

1048
01:04:36,304 --> 01:04:39,242
And we want Powers back
for the same reasons.

1049
01:04:42,577 --> 01:04:44,947
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: I think
you are saying, sir,

1050
01:04:44,981 --> 01:04:47,416
that there might be
a happy ending for everyone.

1051
01:04:47,449 --> 01:04:50,418
Yes, if we, um,
indulge their fiction.

1052
01:04:58,594 --> 01:05:01,030
We want you
to negotiate the swap

1053
01:05:01,063 --> 01:05:02,231
because you're
a private citizen,

1054
01:05:02,264 --> 01:05:04,432
so it's not
governments talking.

1055
01:05:04,467 --> 01:05:07,168
They don't acknowledge
Abel as a Soviet citizen,

1056
01:05:07,202 --> 01:05:10,972
and we don't acknowledge East Germany
as a sovereign country.

1057
01:05:11,007 --> 01:05:12,275
We haven't
recognized East Berlin

1058
01:05:12,307 --> 01:05:15,043
since it was annexed
by the Soviets in '45.

1059
01:05:15,077 --> 01:05:18,280
So I act as a negotiator,
representing...

1060
01:05:18,313 --> 01:05:20,148
You're not
representing anybody.

1061
01:05:20,181 --> 01:05:21,222
Not a government official.

1062
01:05:21,250 --> 01:05:23,418
You have no
official standing.

1063
01:05:23,452 --> 01:05:25,454
So, some fiction
on our side as well.

1064
01:05:25,487 --> 01:05:26,789
Correct.

1065
01:05:26,822 --> 01:05:30,192
Which means, of course,
you can't rely on any acknowledgment

1066
01:05:30,226 --> 01:05:32,994
or help if things go south.

1067
01:05:33,028 --> 01:05:35,097
CIA can't be responsible.

1068
01:05:35,130 --> 01:05:36,431
We can't be embarrassed.

1069
01:05:39,367 --> 01:05:41,503
Where do they want this
negotiation to take place?

1070
01:05:41,536 --> 01:05:43,105
There.

1071
01:05:43,139 --> 01:05:44,340
"There"?

1072
01:05:44,373 --> 01:05:45,541
East Berlin.

1073
01:05:48,143 --> 01:05:50,313
Well, isn't East Berlin
getting rather...

1074
01:05:50,346 --> 01:05:52,547
Oh, yes, the place
is getting complicated.

1075
01:05:54,483 --> 01:05:55,583
Yeah, complicated.

1076
01:05:58,554 --> 01:06:02,057
The Soviet side has been setting up
checkpoints for the past few months

1077
01:06:02,090 --> 01:06:04,393
to try to stop people
hemorrhaging to the Western sectors,

1078
01:06:04,427 --> 01:06:06,262
and it hasn't worked.

1079
01:06:06,295 --> 01:06:08,097
We have intelligence
to suggest

1080
01:06:08,130 --> 01:06:10,266
that they may go
one step further

1081
01:06:10,298 --> 01:06:12,400
and wall off
the entire Eastern sector.

1082
01:06:18,273 --> 01:06:20,509
Okay, that's it
in a nutshell.

1083
01:06:20,543 --> 01:06:23,211
The decision
is entirely yours.

1084
01:06:23,244 --> 01:06:26,315
You need time
to kick it around.

1085
01:06:32,288 --> 01:06:34,523
No.

1086
01:06:34,556 --> 01:06:35,657
No, I don't.

1087
01:06:35,690 --> 01:06:38,594
Well, fine. Fine.

1088
01:06:38,626 --> 01:06:41,197
Best that this all
remains confidential.

1089
01:06:41,230 --> 01:06:46,302
Let's not discuss any of this with, uh,
Mary or with anyone else.

1090
01:06:46,335 --> 01:06:48,471
Share the correspondence
only with us.

1091
01:06:48,503 --> 01:06:50,338
Let us know what they want to do
and when.

1092
01:06:50,373 --> 01:06:53,309
I've got no client,
no wife, no country.

1093
01:06:53,342 --> 01:06:56,411
Don't know what I'm doing
or when or who for.

1094
01:06:56,445 --> 01:06:58,179
You're doing it
for your country,

1095
01:06:58,213 --> 01:07:00,215
but your country
doesn't know that yet.

1096
01:07:00,248 --> 01:07:03,351
What about my client,
the other person in this equation? My guy?

1097
01:07:03,385 --> 01:07:05,253
Your guy?
You mean the Russian?

1098
01:07:05,286 --> 01:07:07,155
He's not your guy anymore,
Counselor.

1099
01:07:07,188 --> 01:07:10,226
Your guy is
Francis Gary Powers now.

1100
01:07:10,259 --> 01:07:13,128
What do I tell
Rudolf Abel?

1101
01:07:13,161 --> 01:07:14,530
Tell him not to drop dead.

1102
01:07:25,141 --> 01:07:26,541
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

1103
01:07:33,648 --> 01:07:35,483
<font color="#80FF00">(MAN SHOUTING IN
OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1104
01:07:38,286 --> 01:07:39,422
<font color="#80FF00">(PANTING)</font>

1105
01:07:39,454 --> 01:07:41,222
<font color="#80FF00">(CHILD CRYING)</font>

1106
01:07:41,257 --> 01:07:43,092
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT SHOUTING
AND YELLING)</font>

1107
01:08:17,359 --> 01:08:19,127
<font color="#80FF00">(BABY WAILING)</font>

1108
01:08:33,609 --> 01:08:35,644
<font color="#80FF00">(CHILDREN CONVERSING
IN GERMAN)</font>

1109
01:08:52,761 --> 01:08:54,563
<font color="#80FF00">(PANTING)</font>

1110
01:09:04,472 --> 01:09:07,475
<font color="#80FF00">(MAN ON RADIO SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1111
01:09:07,510 --> 01:09:08,577
Professor?

1112
01:09:14,483 --> 01:09:15,484
Professor?

1113
01:09:16,484 --> 01:09:18,453
Papa went to university.

1114
01:09:18,487 --> 01:09:20,655
You shouldn't be here,
Frederic.

1115
01:09:20,689 --> 01:09:22,557
They're having
classes today?

1116
01:09:22,591 --> 01:09:24,827
He went to see.
That's where he goes.

1117
01:09:24,859 --> 01:09:26,427
What are you doing here?

1118
01:09:26,462 --> 01:09:28,463
I came to get you
and your father.

1119
01:09:28,497 --> 01:09:30,498
You have to come with me.
You might not get out tomorrow.

1120
01:09:30,533 --> 01:09:31,734
Just... Just leave?

1121
01:09:31,766 --> 01:09:34,203
Yes. Yes, yes, yes, leave.
Right now.

1122
01:09:35,337 --> 01:09:37,206
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1123
01:10:05,367 --> 01:10:07,735
<font color="#ffff00">PRYOR</font>: Go back. Go back.

1124
01:10:07,770 --> 01:10:10,305
I'll be okay, I'm American.
Go back.

1125
01:10:10,338 --> 01:10:12,574
Go back to your father's.
We'll find another way. Go.

1126
01:10:12,608 --> 01:10:14,443
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1127
01:10:18,413 --> 01:10:19,414
Hey!

1128
01:10:19,782 --> 01:10:21,382
<font color="#ffff00">PRYOR</font>: So, hi.

1129
01:10:24,653 --> 01:10:26,488
Papers, please.

1130
01:10:26,521 --> 01:10:28,623
<i>Ich bin Student</i>
Frederic Pryor.

1131
01:10:28,657 --> 01:10:30,826
Yes,
<i>Student.</i>

1132
01:10:30,859 --> 01:10:33,295
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(GRUNTS)</font>

1133
01:10:33,328 --> 01:10:34,762
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPS)</font>

1134
01:10:34,796 --> 01:10:36,599
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1135
01:10:41,804 --> 01:10:44,340
My name's Frederic Pryor.
I'm an economics student.

1136
01:10:44,372 --> 01:10:46,408
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1137
01:10:46,442 --> 01:10:50,511
See? <font color="#80FF00">(GASPS)</font>
It's my dissertation. My thesis.

1138
01:10:50,546 --> 01:10:52,581
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1139
01:10:52,614 --> 01:10:54,082
You see? Look, look.

1140
01:10:54,115 --> 01:10:57,720
Look "The foreign trade system
of the European Communist nations." Huh?

1141
01:10:57,752 --> 01:10:59,087
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1142
01:11:02,890 --> 01:11:04,659
It was a lot of work.

1143
01:11:04,693 --> 01:11:06,462
<font color="#80FF00">(RAPID BREATHING)</font>

1144
01:11:09,832 --> 01:11:11,800
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1145
01:11:11,833 --> 01:11:14,536
No, that's my only copy.
<font color="#80FF00">(GRUNTS)</font>

1146
01:11:14,569 --> 01:11:15,871
<font color="#80FF00">(SHOUTING IN GERMAN)</font>
<font color="#80FF00">(DOG BARKING)</font>

1147
01:11:15,904 --> 01:11:17,573
No! <font color="#80FF00">(CRYING)</font>

1148
01:11:19,774 --> 01:11:21,377
<font color="#ffff00">MARY</font>:
<i>Tell me something.</i>

1149
01:11:21,409 --> 01:11:23,479
It came up
at the last minute.

1150
01:11:23,511 --> 01:11:25,814
They have offices
in London.

1151
01:11:25,847 --> 01:11:28,817
These clients.
Not my idea.

1152
01:11:28,850 --> 01:11:31,487
Scotland, this
fishing expedition.

1153
01:11:31,520 --> 01:11:33,721
What kind of
fishing trip, Jim?

1154
01:11:33,756 --> 01:11:36,492
Salmon fishing.

1155
01:11:36,524 --> 01:11:38,564
Just tell me that you're
not gonna be in any danger,

1156
01:11:38,594 --> 01:11:39,927
that this is
just a routine business trip,

1157
01:11:39,962 --> 01:11:41,663
and I won't worry.

1158
01:11:42,398 --> 01:11:43,666
I need my passport.

1159
01:11:48,804 --> 01:11:50,471
Just give me something
to hold on to.

1160
01:11:50,506 --> 01:11:51,907
I don't even care
if it's the truth.

1161
01:11:53,942 --> 01:11:55,610
I'm doing this for us.

1162
01:12:19,033 --> 01:12:21,002
<font color="#ffff00">HOFFMAN</font>: Mr. Michener, here,
is your Berlin escort.

1163
01:12:21,036 --> 01:12:23,838
He's an attache
with the U.S. embassy.

1164
01:12:23,872 --> 01:12:26,475
So, it's gotten goddamn complicated.
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Uh-huh.

1165
01:12:26,507 --> 01:12:27,876
As you know,
the last letter from Mrs. Abel,

1166
01:12:27,909 --> 01:12:32,511
whoever she is, talks about
a lawyer in East Berlin,
Mr. Vogel, whoever he is,
representing her interests, she says.

1168
01:12:32,547 --> 01:12:33,916
Yeah, Vogel says

1169
01:12:33,948 --> 01:12:35,850
he's gone to the Russian
embassy in East Berlin

1170
01:12:35,884 --> 01:12:38,520
and they may be ready
to trade Abel for Powers.

1171
01:12:38,554 --> 01:12:40,456
But no one knows who's who,

1172
01:12:40,488 --> 01:12:41,724
but essentially they're all Russian,
right?

1173
01:12:41,756 --> 01:12:44,560
Well, Soviet.
Well, we think so.

1174
01:12:44,592 --> 01:12:46,761
What do you mean?
Could we turn up the heat in here?

1175
01:12:46,795 --> 01:12:48,596
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1176
01:12:48,630 --> 01:12:50,998
There's a wrinkle.

1177
01:12:51,033 --> 01:12:53,469
They might try
to throw you a curveball.

1178
01:12:53,502 --> 01:12:56,471
Try and get you to accept
another prisoner other than Powers.

1179
01:12:56,505 --> 01:12:57,840
Another American?

1180
01:12:57,872 --> 01:13:01,009
The, uh, East German Stasi
picked up an American student.

1181
01:13:01,042 --> 01:13:02,243
Frederic Pryor.

1182
01:13:02,277 --> 01:13:04,846
Uh, the kid they found on the wrong
side of the Wall.

1183
01:13:04,880 --> 01:13:06,648
He's a grad student
out of Yale.

1184
01:13:06,681 --> 01:13:08,716
Came over here to study economics.
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Mmm-hmm.

1185
01:13:08,751 --> 01:13:12,587
So, they're making noises
about trying this kid for espionage.

1186
01:13:12,621 --> 01:13:15,056
Grave offense, capital crime.
Well.

1187
01:13:15,090 --> 01:13:19,828
Vogel got in touch with us,
said he represents Pryor.

1188
01:13:19,862 --> 01:13:24,800
Wait a minute. Vogel represents Mrs. Abel
and this... This kid?

1189
01:13:24,832 --> 01:13:26,502
Yeah, he's busy.

1190
01:13:27,636 --> 01:13:28,703
Well, so,
what is he?

1191
01:13:28,737 --> 01:13:31,073
Vogel, is he, uh,
East German or is he Russian?

1192
01:13:31,105 --> 01:13:33,775
Well, try to figure it out
when you meet him.

1193
01:13:33,809 --> 01:13:36,078
At Russian embassy,
East Berlin, noon tomorrow.

1194
01:13:36,110 --> 01:13:38,814
Hoffman will show you
how to get there.

1195
01:13:38,846 --> 01:13:40,916
Wait, show me how to get there?
I'm gonna have an escort.

1196
01:13:40,948 --> 01:13:42,784
Right?

1197
01:13:42,818 --> 01:13:44,920
Well, that was the plan
but like I said, there's a wrinkle.

1198
01:13:44,953 --> 01:13:47,689
I won't be taking you.
Decision's been made.

1199
01:13:47,722 --> 01:13:50,492
No U.S. government personnel are to
cross the border until further notice.

1200
01:13:50,525 --> 01:13:51,827
How come?
It's too dangerous.

1201
01:13:51,859 --> 01:13:53,694
Yeah, the East Germans
grabbed this kid.

1202
01:13:53,729 --> 01:13:57,666
There's a degree of impunity
that we had not anticipated.

1203
01:13:57,698 --> 01:14:00,668
The point is that the East German agenda
and the Russian agenda

1204
01:14:00,702 --> 01:14:02,104
may not be
the same thing.

1205
01:14:02,136 --> 01:14:04,006
Try and figure it out
and act accordingly.

1206
01:14:04,038 --> 01:14:05,841
And try to make a deal.
Powers for Abel.

1207
01:14:05,873 --> 01:14:08,777
Well, what about this
college kid, Pryor?

1208
01:14:08,810 --> 01:14:11,613
Mmm-mmm.
Don't fall for that.

1209
01:14:11,646 --> 01:14:12,713
We can get him out
another time.

1210
01:14:12,747 --> 01:14:13,948
Powers is the whole
ball game.

1211
01:14:37,005 --> 01:14:38,707
Where's your room?

1212
01:14:39,707 --> 01:14:40,868
I'll be staying
at the Hilton.

1213
01:14:43,110 --> 01:14:44,110
It's not far.

1214
01:14:46,180 --> 01:14:48,750
This is a number here
in West Berlin.

1215
01:14:48,783 --> 01:14:50,144
Memorize it and
give it back to me.

1216
01:14:51,787 --> 01:14:53,020
No, no. I...
I mean, now.

1217
01:14:55,756 --> 01:14:57,759
The Soviets keep cutting
the international lines

1218
01:14:57,792 --> 01:15:00,028
but local calls
are okay.

1219
01:15:00,061 --> 01:15:01,896
Someone will pick up
day and night.

1220
01:15:10,738 --> 01:15:14,076
Now, this docket attached to your passport
will serve as your ticket

1221
01:15:14,109 --> 01:15:16,011
into and out of
the Eastern Soviet sector.

1222
01:15:18,113 --> 01:15:19,993
The meeting with Vogel
is set for noon tomorrow.

1223
01:15:26,755 --> 01:15:28,657
There are only a few routes
left to the East.

1224
01:15:28,689 --> 01:15:30,893
You take the S-Bahn, the station we showed
you on the way in.

1225
01:15:30,926 --> 01:15:33,194
You're gonna get off
at the Friedrichstrasse station here

1226
01:15:33,228 --> 01:15:36,131
and walk to the Soviet embassy
on Unter den Linden here.

1227
01:15:36,163 --> 01:15:38,800
Look at the map now.
You shouldn't take it with you.

1228
01:15:38,833 --> 01:15:40,935
You're looking at a map,
you're an American, you're a spy.

1229
01:15:40,969 --> 01:15:42,970
You could
well be detained.

1230
01:15:43,005 --> 01:15:45,073
Just avoid interacting
with people generally.

1231
01:15:45,106 --> 01:15:48,042
You don't...
You don't belong, so don't stick out.

1232
01:15:48,076 --> 01:15:50,177
Food is scarce over there
and things have started to fall apart.

1233
01:15:50,212 --> 01:15:51,412
There are gangs,

1234
01:15:51,445 --> 01:15:54,716
and rule of law is less firmly
established over there.

1235
01:15:54,750 --> 01:15:56,752
And definitely stay away
from the Wall.

1236
01:15:56,784 --> 01:15:57,953
On their side,
there's a line

1237
01:15:57,985 --> 01:15:59,020
cleared of buildings
along the Wall.

1238
01:15:59,054 --> 01:16:00,756
They call it
the "Death Line."

1239
01:16:00,788 --> 01:16:02,690
Cross it and
you'll be shot.

1240
01:16:02,724 --> 01:16:05,060
Is there any outcome here
where I'm not either detained or shot?

1241
01:16:05,092 --> 01:16:07,695
Don't worry too much
about what Michener said.

1242
01:16:07,729 --> 01:16:09,765
No, I'm worried about
what you're saying.

1243
01:16:09,797 --> 01:16:11,800
This kid, Pryor,

1244
01:16:11,832 --> 01:16:13,234
that the East Germans have,
how old is he?

1245
01:16:13,268 --> 01:16:15,671
Twenty-five. Why?

1246
01:16:15,703 --> 01:16:16,872
Same age as Dougie.

1247
01:16:19,207 --> 01:16:20,708
Who?

1248
01:16:20,742 --> 01:16:22,210
Eh, an associate of mine.

1249
01:16:24,680 --> 01:16:25,814
Frederic Pryor.

1250
01:16:28,082 --> 01:16:29,952
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1251
01:17:11,926 --> 01:17:14,863
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)</font>

1252
01:17:31,179 --> 01:17:32,279
<font color="#80FF00">(JAMES SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1253
01:17:58,840 --> 01:18:00,307
<font color="#80FF00">(BOTH SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1254
01:18:03,011 --> 01:18:04,145
Okay, okay, okay.

1255
01:18:04,179 --> 01:18:05,780
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1256
01:18:40,948 --> 01:18:42,016
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

1257
01:18:46,822 --> 01:18:48,155
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1258
01:18:51,960 --> 01:18:53,095
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: <i>Danke.</i>

1259
01:19:26,060 --> 01:19:28,296
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1260
01:19:50,252 --> 01:19:56,024
So, we apologize
if this offends.

1261
01:19:57,091 --> 01:19:58,426
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1262
01:20:01,363 --> 01:20:03,131
Uh... <font color="#80FF00">(SNAPS FINGERS)</font>
Coat?

1263
01:20:04,231 --> 01:20:06,167
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1264
01:20:08,470 --> 01:20:09,970
<font color="#80FF00">(SNICKERING)</font>

1265
01:20:22,384 --> 01:20:23,385
Okay.

1266
01:20:24,920 --> 01:20:27,087
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1267
01:20:51,546 --> 01:20:53,280
<font color="#80FF00">(SHIVERING)</font>

1268
01:20:54,015 --> 01:20:55,182
<font color="#80FF00">(COUGHING AND SNIFFLING)</font>

1269
01:21:16,971 --> 01:21:20,007
Do you understand English?
Yes, sir.

1270
01:21:20,041 --> 01:21:22,143
I'm James Donovan.
I'm here to meet a Mr. Vogel.

1271
01:21:22,176 --> 01:21:24,279
Mr. Vogel.
He doesn't work here.

1272
01:21:24,311 --> 01:21:26,231
I'm just supposed to meet him.
That's all I know.

1273
01:21:49,604 --> 01:21:51,238
Mr. Donovan!

1274
01:21:52,106 --> 01:21:53,440
So we finally meet you.

1275
01:21:53,475 --> 01:21:55,010
<font color="#80FF00">(EXCLAIMING)</font>

1276
01:21:55,609 --> 01:21:57,244
I'm Helen Abel.

1277
01:21:57,279 --> 01:21:58,579
How is our husband?
Oh. He's fine.

1278
01:21:58,613 --> 01:22:00,081
How is my Rudolf?

1279
01:22:00,114 --> 01:22:02,050
As good as
can be expected.

1280
01:22:02,082 --> 01:22:04,586
And we will
get him back, yes?

1281
01:22:04,618 --> 01:22:07,055
This is our daughter.
How do you do?

1282
01:22:07,454 --> 01:22:08,454
Lydia.

1283
01:22:09,090 --> 01:22:10,292
Are you Mr. Vogel?

1284
01:22:11,225 --> 01:22:12,560
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLING)</font>
No, no, no!

1285
01:22:12,594 --> 01:22:14,328
I'm not Vogel.

1286
01:22:14,362 --> 01:22:15,529
I'm cousin Drews.

1287
01:22:17,465 --> 01:22:19,333
This is Rudolf's
beloved cousin Drews.

1288
01:22:19,367 --> 01:22:21,569
Yes, cousin Drews.
I am cousin Drews.

1289
01:22:21,603 --> 01:22:23,305
Cousin Drews, yes.

1290
01:22:23,337 --> 01:22:24,372
I'm Helen Abel.

1291
01:22:28,510 --> 01:22:29,577
When will you see him?

1292
01:22:31,345 --> 01:22:33,347
When will your government
release him?

1293
01:22:33,381 --> 01:22:35,350
I'm a little,
uh, lost here.

1294
01:22:35,382 --> 01:22:37,252
I'm supposed to meet
a Mr. Vogel.

1295
01:22:37,284 --> 01:22:39,386
<font color="#ffff00">BOTH</font>: Oh, Mr. Vogel
is our lawyer.

1296
01:22:39,421 --> 01:22:43,358
We were supposed to negotiate,
uh, the exchange.

1297
01:22:43,390 --> 01:22:46,161
To bring Rudolf here?
Yes, yes, to bring him here.

1298
01:22:46,194 --> 01:22:49,029
But it doesn't happen unilaterally,
do you understand?

1299
01:22:49,064 --> 01:22:50,564
It has to be
an exchange.

1300
01:22:52,266 --> 01:22:53,467
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1301
01:22:54,202 --> 01:22:55,369
Not here?

1302
01:22:56,537 --> 01:22:57,604
<font color="#80FF00">(SOBBING)</font>

1303
01:22:59,140 --> 01:23:00,574
<font color="#80FF00">(LYDIA SPEAKING
IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1304
01:23:02,476 --> 01:23:03,511
<font color="#ffff00">LYDIA</font>: Shh.

1305
01:23:03,545 --> 01:23:05,212
<font color="#80FF00">(LYDIA SPEAKING
IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1306
01:23:05,579 --> 01:23:07,381
Shh. Shh.

1307
01:23:07,414 --> 01:23:09,417
<font color="#80FF00">(LYDIA SPEAKING
IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1308
01:23:09,451 --> 01:23:11,386
<font color="#80FF00">(DREWS CONSOLING
IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1309
01:23:15,689 --> 01:23:17,292
Mr. Donovan?

1310
01:23:17,324 --> 01:23:18,460
Mr. Vogel.

1311
01:23:18,493 --> 01:23:20,395
No, no, no.
Goodness.

1312
01:23:20,427 --> 01:23:22,529
My name is Schischkin.
Ivan Schischkin.

1313
01:23:22,564 --> 01:23:24,631
I'm the second secretary
of the Soviet embassy.

1314
01:23:24,666 --> 01:23:25,766
<font color="#80FF00">(SIGHS)</font>

1315
01:23:25,800 --> 01:23:29,170
It wouldn't be appropriate
for Mr. Vogel to join us.

1316
01:23:29,203 --> 01:23:31,372
He is a German national.

1317
01:23:31,405 --> 01:23:33,608
So you aren't here to...

1318
01:23:33,640 --> 01:23:37,212
To facilitate this meeting.
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLES)</font>

1319
01:23:37,244 --> 01:23:40,314
Uh, perhaps Mrs. Abel

1320
01:23:40,347 --> 01:23:42,516
would like to compose
herself privately?

1321
01:23:42,550 --> 01:23:45,520
And let Mr. Donovan
and myself to talk?

1322
01:23:47,622 --> 01:23:48,622
Thank you.

1323
01:23:56,131 --> 01:23:57,131
<font color="#80FF00">(DOOR CLOSES)</font>

1324
01:23:58,533 --> 01:24:01,235
So, I'm confused.
Yes.

1325
01:24:01,269 --> 01:24:02,703
Uh, I don't care
who I talk to

1326
01:24:02,737 --> 01:24:06,207
if it's about
an exchange of personnel.

1327
01:24:06,240 --> 01:24:08,208
Are you the person
to talk to?

1328
01:24:08,243 --> 01:24:11,179
Yes, yes, yes, of course.
Uh, we should talk.

1329
01:24:11,212 --> 01:24:13,448
Please, have a seat,
Mr. Donovan.

1330
01:24:13,480 --> 01:24:15,115
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING AND COUGHING)</font>

1331
01:24:15,149 --> 01:24:17,351
Uh, how did you arrive
to the Eastern sector?

1332
01:24:17,385 --> 01:24:18,453
The train.
The S-Bahn.

1333
01:24:18,485 --> 01:24:20,255
Ah, and you were alone?

1334
01:24:20,287 --> 01:24:21,722
Yeah.

1335
01:24:21,756 --> 01:24:25,126
And you are
credentialed?

1336
01:24:25,159 --> 01:24:27,127
Now, you must know
about me, sir.

1337
01:24:27,162 --> 01:24:28,662
I'm a private citizen.

1338
01:24:28,695 --> 01:24:30,664
I'm a lawyer in Brooklyn
in the United States,

1339
01:24:30,698 --> 01:24:33,333
and I've taken time
out of my busy schedule

1340
01:24:33,368 --> 01:24:36,136
to help my client,
Rudolf Abel.

1341
01:24:36,171 --> 01:24:40,207
I'm authorized to arrange
an exchange of Abel

1342
01:24:40,240 --> 01:24:42,676
for Francis Gary Powers.

1343
01:24:42,710 --> 01:24:45,579
This is a full
pardon of Abel

1344
01:24:45,613 --> 01:24:47,932
that will be signed
when the exchange actually takes place.

1345
01:24:49,250 --> 01:24:50,618
This is the only
reason I'm here.

1346
01:24:50,652 --> 01:24:53,421
I hope and expect to make
this arrangement quickly.

1347
01:24:54,488 --> 01:24:56,524
Abel for Francis
Gary Powers

1348
01:24:58,460 --> 01:25:00,194
and Frederic Pryor.

1349
01:25:02,463 --> 01:25:04,466
Mmm, we don't have Pryor.

1350
01:25:04,498 --> 01:25:06,467
No? You don't?

1351
01:25:08,770 --> 01:25:11,439
I've heard of Pryor,
but he's held

1352
01:25:11,472 --> 01:25:14,609
by the German
Democratic Republic,

1353
01:25:14,641 --> 01:25:17,479
not by the Union of Soviet
Socialist Republics.

1354
01:25:18,412 --> 01:25:21,381
Well, I'll tell you
the first problem.

1355
01:25:21,416 --> 01:25:24,418
The names of your countries
are too long. <font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1356
01:25:24,452 --> 01:25:27,521
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>
Yes, yes.

1357
01:25:27,555 --> 01:25:28,590
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLES AND COUGHS)</font>

1358
01:25:28,622 --> 01:25:30,591
If we release Powers,

1359
01:25:30,625 --> 01:25:32,594
it is only to
promote a goodwill

1360
01:25:32,627 --> 01:25:33,761
between our countries.

1361
01:25:35,529 --> 01:25:38,166
So, it cannot be
an exchange.

1362
01:25:41,402 --> 01:25:42,604
Perhaps...

1363
01:25:42,636 --> 01:25:45,506
Perhaps you could
release Abel

1364
01:25:45,539 --> 01:25:47,475
as a token of goodwill
to our friends in Germany,

1365
01:25:47,509 --> 01:25:50,377
and then,
some months later,

1366
01:25:50,411 --> 01:25:52,814
we would release Powers.

1367
01:25:52,846 --> 01:25:55,283
There is an idea.
No. That just won't work for us at all.

1368
01:25:55,315 --> 01:25:57,618
See, we need this to be
an exchange.

1369
01:25:57,652 --> 01:25:59,420
You can call it
what you want,

1370
01:25:59,453 --> 01:26:01,288
but an exchange
it must be.

1371
01:26:01,322 --> 01:26:04,458
We can have Abel here
within 48 hours.

1372
01:26:04,492 --> 01:26:07,662
We need Powers
at the same time we give you Abel.

1373
01:26:09,863 --> 01:26:12,634
Hmm. This is...

1374
01:26:12,667 --> 01:26:16,570
Can we call this
the "impatient plan"?

1375
01:26:16,604 --> 01:26:18,439
Well, you can call it
whatever you want.

1376
01:26:19,373 --> 01:26:20,574
<font color="#80FF00">(EXHALES)</font>

1377
01:26:20,608 --> 01:26:23,444
I can relay your
impatient plan to Moscow,

1378
01:26:23,478 --> 01:26:24,345
see what they say...
<font color="#80FF00">(COUGHING)</font>

1379
01:26:24,378 --> 01:26:27,715
But they will wonder
why so impatient.

1380
01:26:29,317 --> 01:26:30,717
Because I have a cold.

1381
01:26:30,752 --> 01:26:32,671
And I don't live in Berlin
and I want to go home.

1383
01:26:33,621 --> 01:26:37,591
They will think,
"Well, the Americans
must have gotten"

1384
01:26:37,625 --> 01:26:40,561
"all the information
that Abel had to give."

1385
01:26:40,595 --> 01:26:44,399
"And now they're impatient
to trade him,"

1386
01:26:44,431 --> 01:26:47,467
"hoping to get in return
this man, Powers,"

1387
01:26:47,502 --> 01:26:49,604
"that, perhaps,
has not yet given up"

1388
01:26:49,636 --> 01:26:51,639
"all the information
that he has to give."

1389
01:26:53,507 --> 01:26:55,643
This is not
an equitable trade, sir.

1390
01:26:56,911 --> 01:26:58,546
But what you're saying is,

1391
01:26:58,579 --> 01:27:00,582
if Powers has given up
everything he knows,

1392
01:27:00,614 --> 01:27:02,850
then Moscow would trade?

1393
01:27:02,884 --> 01:27:04,452
Why wouldn't they?

1394
01:27:05,619 --> 01:27:08,655
As for Abel, if he dies
in an American prison,

1395
01:27:08,689 --> 01:27:11,359
the next Russian operative
who gets caught

1396
01:27:11,391 --> 01:27:13,427
might think twice about
keeping his mouth shut.

1397
01:27:13,461 --> 01:27:18,800
And you never know.
Abel might want to see the sky again

1398
01:27:18,832 --> 01:27:21,868
and decide to trade Russian
secrets for small American favors.

1399
01:27:25,707 --> 01:27:27,774
How can we know this?

1400
01:27:27,809 --> 01:27:31,479
We little men,
we just do our jobs.

1401
01:27:31,511 --> 01:27:33,847
Like Lieutenant Powers.
He's just a pilot.

1402
01:27:36,684 --> 01:27:40,520
He was making photographs
from 70,000 feet

1403
01:27:40,555 --> 01:27:41,715
when he was shot
from the sky.

1404
01:27:42,689 --> 01:27:44,759
People in my country
consider this

1405
01:27:44,792 --> 01:27:46,627
an act of war.

1406
01:27:48,962 --> 01:27:51,733
We have to get off this
merry-go-round, sir.

1407
01:27:51,765 --> 01:27:53,533
The next mistake
our countries make

1408
01:27:53,568 --> 01:27:55,336
could be the last one.

1409
01:27:56,537 --> 01:27:58,805
We need to have
the conversation

1410
01:27:58,840 --> 01:28:00,541
our governments can't.

1411
01:28:05,846 --> 01:28:08,515
I will ask Moscow.

1412
01:28:08,548 --> 01:28:10,518
Who knows
what they will say.

1413
01:28:12,453 --> 01:28:13,787
There are a lot of people,
Mr. Donovan,

1414
01:28:13,820 --> 01:28:17,925
who doesn't want this
exchange to ever take place.

1415
01:28:20,862 --> 01:28:24,399
Can you come back tomorrow
to discuss their answer?

1416
01:28:24,431 --> 01:28:25,432
Powers for Abel.

1417
01:28:25,466 --> 01:28:26,934
And Frederic Pryor.

1418
01:28:26,967 --> 01:28:28,469
As I said,

1419
01:28:28,502 --> 01:28:30,671
Pryor is in the hands
of the organs of.

1420
01:28:30,704 --> 01:28:32,907
State Security of the German
Democratic Republic.

1421
01:28:32,939 --> 01:28:35,510
I'm confident you can
make arrangements.

1422
01:28:35,542 --> 01:28:37,845
I am merely a secretary

1423
01:28:37,878 --> 01:28:39,579
in the embassy
of foreign power.

1424
01:28:39,613 --> 01:28:40,948
Okay, okay.

1425
01:28:40,981 --> 01:28:44,585
You have to see Mr. Vogel.
I'll give you his address.

1426
01:28:44,618 --> 01:28:46,554
There actually is
a Mr. Vogel?

1427
01:28:46,587 --> 01:28:48,855
Why would you
imagine otherwise?

1428
01:28:48,890 --> 01:28:50,792
Uh, it's a short cab ride.

1429
01:28:51,993 --> 01:28:54,828
And please, Mr. Donovan,

1430
01:28:54,862 --> 01:28:56,698
wear an overcoat
in this weather.

1431
01:28:58,498 --> 01:28:59,701
I had mine
stolen from me.

1432
01:29:01,034 --> 01:29:02,703
What do you expect?

1433
01:29:02,737 --> 01:29:04,572
It was from
Saks Fifth Avenue.

1434
01:29:05,907 --> 01:29:06,907
Wasn't it?

1435
01:29:09,844 --> 01:29:11,712
<font color="#80FF00">(MAN ON RADIO
SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1436
01:29:15,649 --> 01:29:16,984
<font color="#ffff00">VOGEL</font>: Mr. Donovan.
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>

1437
01:29:17,018 --> 01:29:18,453
Mr. Vogel.

1438
01:29:18,485 --> 01:29:20,454
<font color="#ffff00">VOGEL</font>: Please come in.

1439
01:29:20,488 --> 01:29:21,588
<i>Danke.</i>

1440
01:29:24,958 --> 01:29:26,728
Sorry about the embassy.

1441
01:29:28,796 --> 01:29:30,731
But the Soviets decided
not to host me.

1442
01:29:32,533 --> 01:29:34,435
Very irritating.

1443
01:29:34,469 --> 01:29:36,770
I am still trying
to grasp each party's...

1444
01:29:37,738 --> 01:29:39,506
Status?

1445
01:29:39,539 --> 01:29:42,643
Yes. And interest
in the proceedings.

1446
01:29:42,676 --> 01:29:43,744
Oh.

1447
01:29:43,778 --> 01:29:45,712
It's a new world.

1448
01:29:45,747 --> 01:29:47,782
All very disorienting,
isn't it?

1449
01:29:58,091 --> 01:29:59,827
Let me tell you
what I have to offer.

1450
01:30:01,595 --> 01:30:02,662
<font color="#80FF00">(TURNS OFF RADIO)</font>

1451
01:30:02,697 --> 01:30:04,532
I'm a good friend...

1452
01:30:04,564 --> 01:30:06,600
"Friend."
No, he's older.

1453
01:30:06,634 --> 01:30:08,536
"Protege"?

1454
01:30:08,569 --> 01:30:11,037
Of the attorney general
of the German Democratic Republic.

1455
01:30:11,072 --> 01:30:12,472
Sit down, please.

1456
01:30:15,676 --> 01:30:17,545
This unfortunate,

1457
01:30:17,578 --> 01:30:19,780
Frederic Pryor,

1458
01:30:19,814 --> 01:30:22,716
he was in the wrong place
at the wrong time.

1459
01:30:22,750 --> 01:30:25,819
But every accident,
properly viewed,

1460
01:30:25,853 --> 01:30:27,954
is an opportunity,
isn't it, Mr. Donovan?

1461
01:30:27,988 --> 01:30:30,957
Well...
Your country refuses to recognize

1462
01:30:30,992 --> 01:30:33,661
the German
Democratic Republic.

1463
01:30:33,694 --> 01:30:35,695
They prefer to
make up stories

1464
01:30:35,729 --> 01:30:37,329
that the GDR doesn't
exist, for instance.

1465
01:30:39,699 --> 01:30:41,735
Sorry.

1466
01:30:41,769 --> 01:30:44,104
As a means of forcing your
government's recognition

1467
01:30:44,137 --> 01:30:46,673
of this real place, sir,

1468
01:30:46,707 --> 01:30:48,576
in which you
find yourself,

1469
01:30:48,609 --> 01:30:50,077
the GDR,

1470
01:30:50,110 --> 01:30:52,579
the attorney general is
happy to negotiate with you

1471
01:30:52,613 --> 01:30:54,849
as a representative
of your government

1472
01:30:54,881 --> 01:30:56,117
for Mr. Pryor's return.

1473
01:30:56,149 --> 01:30:57,717
Mr. Vogel,
I'm not here

1474
01:30:57,752 --> 01:30:59,787
as a representative
of my government.

1475
01:31:02,856 --> 01:31:03,856
All right.

1476
01:31:05,059 --> 01:31:06,828
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1477
01:31:06,860 --> 01:31:09,029
I think that's childish,
Mr. Donovan.

1478
01:31:09,063 --> 01:31:11,698
Well, I... I have
no official status.

1479
01:31:11,731 --> 01:31:13,100
Fine.

1480
01:31:13,134 --> 01:31:15,936
You do not represent the USA,
I do not represent the GDR.

1481
01:31:15,970 --> 01:31:18,104
My client is Lydia Abel,
Rudolf's wife.

1482
01:31:18,139 --> 01:31:19,739
And I have also...

1483
01:31:19,774 --> 01:31:21,842
Lydia is the daughter.
Helen is the wife.

1484
01:31:27,782 --> 01:31:28,815
Well...

1485
01:31:30,685 --> 01:31:32,086
I am prepared to offer

1486
01:31:32,118 --> 01:31:34,988
Frederic Pryor's exchange
for Rudolf Abel's.

1487
01:31:35,021 --> 01:31:36,690
<font color="#80FF00">(PUTS GLASS DOWN)</font>

1488
01:31:36,724 --> 01:31:38,024
Then let's be clear.

1489
01:31:38,059 --> 01:31:40,161
We will produce
Rudolf Abel.

1490
01:31:40,194 --> 01:31:43,564
You will produce,
at the same time, the student, Pryor.

1491
01:31:44,097 --> 01:31:45,600
At the same time?

1492
01:31:45,632 --> 01:31:47,068
Absolutely.

1493
01:31:47,100 --> 01:31:51,671
The exchange on offer
is a Russian at the end of his life

1494
01:31:53,975 --> 01:31:56,743
for an American student
at the start of his.

1495
01:32:03,684 --> 01:32:06,654
Schischkin? He's not
an embassy secretary.

1496
01:32:06,686 --> 01:32:09,657
Ivan Schischkin is the KGB's
chief in western Europe.

1497
01:32:09,689 --> 01:32:10,992
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>
Well, whatever he is,

1498
01:32:11,024 --> 01:32:13,927
he's relaying our
proposal to Moscow

1499
01:32:15,762 --> 01:32:16,930
and they'll decide.

1500
01:32:16,963 --> 01:32:19,734
Could I borrow a coat?
I lost mine.

1501
01:32:19,766 --> 01:32:21,202
How did you
lose your coat?

1502
01:32:21,234 --> 01:32:23,104
You know, spy stuff.

1503
01:32:25,706 --> 01:32:28,708
I'll get you another coat.
Good, because... <font color="#80FF00">(EXHALING)</font>

1504
01:32:28,743 --> 01:32:30,912
So who is, uh...
Who is this Vogel?

1505
01:32:31,811 --> 01:32:33,613
Him, we don't know.

1506
01:32:33,648 --> 01:32:35,917
He might be what you
said he said he is.

1507
01:32:35,949 --> 01:32:37,184
Friend of
the attorney general.

1508
01:32:37,217 --> 01:32:40,921
Just some guy the GDR
tapped to handle this.

1509
01:32:40,954 --> 01:32:42,856
Sounds like
the East Germans

1510
01:32:42,890 --> 01:32:45,626
are fighting for
a place at our table.

1511
01:32:45,659 --> 01:32:48,695
The GDR gets Abel back
for the Russians,

1512
01:32:48,729 --> 01:32:49,896
winning their respect...
<font color="#80FF00">(COUGHING)</font>

1513
01:32:49,930 --> 01:32:52,699
And capturing the headlines
for their cause.

1514
01:32:52,733 --> 01:32:54,969
Which, of course,
puts East Germany on the map.

1515
01:32:55,002 --> 01:32:57,704
But we don't care
about their map.

1516
01:32:57,738 --> 01:32:59,206
Stick with
the Russians.

1517
01:32:59,239 --> 01:33:01,841
Stick with the Russians.
It's Powers for Abel.

1518
01:33:01,876 --> 01:33:05,011
So, does all this mean
this might actually happen?

1519
01:33:05,046 --> 01:33:06,913
Yeah, that's what
you seem to be telling me.

1520
01:33:06,948 --> 01:33:09,984
We'll put Abel on a plane,
he'll be here Friday.

1521
01:33:10,016 --> 01:33:12,019
So, if Schischkin
gets the okay,

1522
01:33:12,052 --> 01:33:14,220
all that's left is the
mechanics of the trade.

1523
01:33:14,255 --> 01:33:16,090
How we swap our guy
for their guy.

1524
01:33:16,122 --> 01:33:18,225
Our "guys." Two guys.

1525
01:33:18,259 --> 01:33:20,728
Powers and Pryor.

1526
01:33:20,761 --> 01:33:23,698
No, now don't go
bleeding heart on me.

1527
01:33:23,730 --> 01:33:25,932
Powers is the whole
ball game.

1528
01:33:25,966 --> 01:33:27,734
Forget this
Ivy League boy,

1529
01:33:27,768 --> 01:33:29,869
who obviously thought
it was a good idea

1530
01:33:29,904 --> 01:33:32,273
to study Soviet
economics in Berlin

1531
01:33:32,305 --> 01:33:34,007
in the middle
of the Cold War.

1532
01:33:35,775 --> 01:33:37,110
Powers is who we need.

1533
01:33:38,145 --> 01:33:39,947
You don't even
like Powers.

1534
01:33:41,215 --> 01:33:43,884
Everybody hates Powers.

1535
01:33:43,917 --> 01:33:48,122
He didn't kill himself and he let the
commies parade him on television.

1536
01:33:48,154 --> 01:33:50,690
He's the most
hated man in America.

1537
01:33:50,725 --> 01:33:53,226
After Rudolf Abel,
maybe.

1538
01:33:53,260 --> 01:33:54,929
And me.

1539
01:34:03,938 --> 01:34:05,006
Now wake up!

1540
01:34:05,038 --> 01:34:07,007
<font color="#80FF00">(BOTH SHOUTING
IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

1541
01:34:14,047 --> 01:34:15,916
<font color="#ffff00">POWERS</font>: I gotta sleep.

1542
01:34:15,949 --> 01:34:17,251
I just gotta sleep.

1543
01:34:17,283 --> 01:34:18,284
<font color="#80FF00">(GRUNTS)</font>

1544
01:34:20,954 --> 01:34:22,122
I told you,
you can sleep

1545
01:34:22,155 --> 01:34:23,990
once we talk
what we need to talk.

1546
01:34:24,024 --> 01:34:26,860
You must focus on me.

1547
01:34:26,894 --> 01:34:29,262
Your government doesn't care about you.
You know that.

1548
01:34:29,296 --> 01:34:30,497
Any of you.

1549
01:34:30,530 --> 01:34:34,201
You know that twelve
U-2 pilots incurred brain damage?

1550
01:34:34,234 --> 01:34:37,805
They're vegetables because of altitude,
insufficient oxygen.

1551
01:34:37,837 --> 01:34:39,806
But they keep you
flying, Gary.

1552
01:34:39,840 --> 01:34:41,908
You and your friends.

1553
01:34:41,942 --> 01:34:45,345
They gave you this.
A scratcher, yes?

1554
01:34:45,378 --> 01:34:48,149
Potassium cyanide.

1555
01:34:48,181 --> 01:34:51,118
Does that look like
they care about you?

1556
01:34:51,152 --> 01:34:52,219
<font color="#80FF00">(POWERS GROANING)</font>

1557
01:35:16,976 --> 01:35:18,145
<font color="#80FF00">(POWERS GASPING)</font>

1558
01:35:20,213 --> 01:35:23,783
What is the construction
of the destructor unit?

1559
01:35:23,818 --> 01:35:25,853
By what means
is this unit operated?

1560
01:35:25,886 --> 01:35:28,988
With what explosives
is the unit charged,

1561
01:35:29,023 --> 01:35:30,858
and what destruction
is it capable of?

1562
01:35:31,425 --> 01:35:33,394
I don't know.

1563
01:35:33,426 --> 01:35:35,829
The pilots were never shown
any of the equipment.

1564
01:35:35,863 --> 01:35:38,131
How wide far
can the radar

1565
01:35:38,164 --> 01:35:40,067
map an area during
an overflight?

1566
01:35:40,100 --> 01:35:41,101
I don't know,
I don't know!

1567
01:35:41,135 --> 01:35:45,105
Were you making
the overflight on April 9th

1568
01:35:45,139 --> 01:35:47,841
over the industrial area
and MiG bomber base

1569
01:35:47,875 --> 01:35:48,943
at Baranovichi?

1570
01:35:48,975 --> 01:35:50,877
I was not.

1571
01:35:50,911 --> 01:35:52,279
This was my
first overflight.

1572
01:35:52,313 --> 01:35:56,250
I need to know about
engine-out to glide ratio.

1573
01:35:56,283 --> 01:35:59,319
How the plane
gets so high.

1574
01:35:59,353 --> 01:36:04,291
We know it reaches
altitude of 70,000 feet.

1575
01:36:04,324 --> 01:36:06,260
Gary, we know this.

1576
01:36:06,292 --> 01:36:08,895
And I need to know
where the planes have flown.

1577
01:36:08,929 --> 01:36:11,297
Where they
have flown, Gary.

1578
01:36:11,332 --> 01:36:13,000
And we must have
this talk now.

1579
01:36:16,203 --> 01:36:18,139
Now.

1580
01:36:18,171 --> 01:36:20,841
And then you can
sleep a little.

1581
01:36:25,045 --> 01:36:26,846
<font color="#ffff00">CIA AGENT</font>:
<i>Sir, wake up.</i>

1582
01:36:28,282 --> 01:36:29,282
Wake up, sir.

1583
01:36:32,153 --> 01:36:34,121
Sir, can you come with me, please?
Yeah.

1584
01:36:34,154 --> 01:36:36,189
We gotta move.
Yeah, of course.

1585
01:36:36,222 --> 01:36:37,457
Can you come with me, please?
Yeah.

1586
01:36:37,490 --> 01:36:39,059
Okay.

1587
01:36:40,127 --> 01:36:41,929
Got your glasses?
Uh-huh.

1588
01:36:41,962 --> 01:36:45,198
What time is it?
It's late, but we need to talk,

1589
01:36:45,231 --> 01:36:46,832
and then we need to
get on a plane, okay?

1590
01:36:48,068 --> 01:36:50,171
Yeah.
Yep. Upsy-daisy!

1591
01:36:50,203 --> 01:36:52,105
Upsy-daisy.
That's right.

1592
01:36:52,139 --> 01:36:53,407
There we go.
Keep it moving.

1593
01:36:53,439 --> 01:36:54,908
Thank you.

1594
01:36:57,177 --> 01:36:58,878
Other way, other way.
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>

1595
01:36:58,912 --> 01:36:59,947
There we go.

1596
01:36:59,979 --> 01:37:01,215
You say a plane?

1597
01:37:01,247 --> 01:37:03,117
This way, please.
Keep moving.

1598
01:37:03,149 --> 01:37:06,119
<font color="#ffff00">IVAN</font>: <i>So, I received
a favorable decision</i>

1599
01:37:06,153 --> 01:37:08,221
<i>from Moscow
on your proposal</i>

1600
01:37:08,255 --> 01:37:11,225
that we help our friends in the German
Democratic Republic

1601
01:37:11,492 --> 01:37:15,260
by exchanging
Mr. Powers
for Mr. Abel.

1603
01:37:17,264 --> 01:37:18,498
That's swell.

1604
01:37:18,532 --> 01:37:23,103
May I suggest the Glienicke Bridge
as a place of a swap?

1605
01:37:23,136 --> 01:37:24,404
Why not Checkpoint
Charlie?

1606
01:37:24,438 --> 01:37:27,106
<font color="#80FF00">(GRUNTS)</font> We're not
looking for publicity.

1607
01:37:27,140 --> 01:37:29,076
The Glienicke Bridge
is very quiet,

1608
01:37:29,109 --> 01:37:30,377
especially early
in the morning.

1609
01:37:30,411 --> 01:37:33,180
Glienicke Bridge.
I'll run that by our side.

1610
01:37:33,213 --> 01:37:34,981
We can have Abel here
on Friday.

1611
01:37:35,015 --> 01:37:36,317
So, Saturday morning?

1612
01:37:36,350 --> 01:37:38,385
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>
Always impatience, yeah?

1613
01:37:38,418 --> 01:37:40,054
I want to get home
and get into bed.

1614
01:37:40,087 --> 01:37:42,355
So, Saturday morning?

1615
01:37:42,389 --> 01:37:43,990
5:30?

1616
01:37:44,024 --> 01:37:45,525
All right.

1617
01:37:45,559 --> 01:37:47,261
We're done here.

1618
01:37:47,293 --> 01:37:48,529
This is a number,

1619
01:37:48,561 --> 01:37:51,998
in case anything
should come up.

1620
01:37:52,032 --> 01:37:53,132
All right.

1621
01:37:53,167 --> 01:37:55,101
Someone will answer
at any hour.

1622
01:37:56,203 --> 01:37:57,503
I don't expect
to use it.

1623
01:37:59,105 --> 01:38:01,408
Shall we toast
the arrangement?

1624
01:38:01,442 --> 01:38:02,976
Why not?

1625
01:38:04,578 --> 01:38:06,113
Armenian brandy.

1626
01:38:06,145 --> 01:38:07,914
Good bracer for your cold.
Thank you.

1627
01:38:09,082 --> 01:38:10,283
Would you mind...

1628
01:38:10,317 --> 01:38:12,152
This is not part
of our business

1629
01:38:12,185 --> 01:38:14,421
but I'd like to ask you
a couple of questions.

1630
01:38:14,454 --> 01:38:16,155
You do not
have to answer.

1631
01:38:16,189 --> 01:38:17,390
I shall answer
what I know.

1632
01:38:18,391 --> 01:38:21,427
I like this guy,
your guy.

1633
01:38:21,462 --> 01:38:24,364
What happens to your guy
when he gets home?

1634
01:38:24,398 --> 01:38:26,399
Well, we have to make
a determination

1635
01:38:26,432 --> 01:38:29,103
whether our guy
is now your guy.

1636
01:38:29,136 --> 01:38:31,005
Now, as I said
to you before,

1637
01:38:31,037 --> 01:38:32,273
he's acted
honorably.

1638
01:38:32,305 --> 01:38:34,041
He's still your guy,
believe me.

1639
01:38:34,073 --> 01:38:37,043
Of course you will
say that if it were true

1640
01:38:37,077 --> 01:38:39,279
and if it were
not true.

1641
01:38:39,313 --> 01:38:41,582
I guess it has
come down to that, huh?

1642
01:38:41,614 --> 01:38:43,317
Well, forgive me
for pushing,

1643
01:38:43,350 --> 01:38:46,185
but is he
in any danger

1644
01:38:46,220 --> 01:38:48,521
if the determination
is made?

1645
01:38:48,555 --> 01:38:50,990
Well, goodness.

1646
01:38:51,024 --> 01:38:53,594
As things are now,
everyone is in danger.

1647
01:38:53,627 --> 01:38:55,194
Cheers.

1648
01:38:55,229 --> 01:38:56,430
<i>Na zdorovie.</i>
<font color="#80FF00">(KNOCKING ON DOOR)</font>

1649
01:38:58,064 --> 01:38:59,300
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING RUSSIAN)</font>

1650
01:39:09,342 --> 01:39:11,278
There will be no exchange
on Glienicke Bridge.

1651
01:39:12,378 --> 01:39:14,314
Excuse me, sir.

1652
01:39:14,347 --> 01:39:16,382
There will be
no exchange.

1653
01:39:17,251 --> 01:39:19,118
Not for Frederic Pryor.

1654
01:39:19,153 --> 01:39:22,256
Mr. Vogel, I thought
yesterday we had agreed.

1655
01:39:22,288 --> 01:39:24,524
We agreed on an exchange,
Abel for Pryor.

1656
01:39:26,260 --> 01:39:29,395
Now I learn you are
a rug merchant

1657
01:39:29,430 --> 01:39:32,298
selling the same rug
to two customers.

1658
01:39:32,332 --> 01:39:35,202
Abel for Pryor you sell to us.
Yeah.

1659
01:39:35,234 --> 01:39:37,438
And Abel for Powers
you sell to the Soviets.

1660
01:39:37,470 --> 01:39:40,106
I'm not quite sure
what the problem is

1661
01:39:40,140 --> 01:39:43,344
if the arrangement satisfies
two parties or three or four.

1662
01:39:43,377 --> 01:39:44,511
What difference
does it make?

1663
01:39:44,545 --> 01:39:46,512
The arrangement does not
satisfy this party.

1664
01:39:51,385 --> 01:39:52,418
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font>
Now, Mr. Vogel, please...

1665
01:39:52,453 --> 01:39:55,389
Obviously you do not know
who you are dealing with.

1666
01:39:57,457 --> 01:40:00,094
Here's the interest
of the German Democratic Republic.

1667
01:40:01,395 --> 01:40:02,595
To deal with,

1668
01:40:02,628 --> 01:40:05,498
and to be seen to be dealing
with another sovereign power,

1669
01:40:05,532 --> 01:40:07,167
the United States.

1670
01:40:07,201 --> 01:40:09,269
An equal power.

1671
01:40:09,302 --> 01:40:10,671
Instead you treat us
as stooges

1672
01:40:10,703 --> 01:40:12,672
for the Union of Soviet
Socialist Republics.

1673
01:40:12,706 --> 01:40:15,109
Can we just call them the Russians?
It'll save time.

1674
01:40:16,577 --> 01:40:18,545
I have an appointment, sir.

1675
01:40:18,578 --> 01:40:20,346
I think it's you
who are wasting time.

1676
01:40:20,381 --> 01:40:22,416
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Now, tell me if
I am describing this wrong.

1677
01:40:22,448 --> 01:40:24,184
You have a kid,
a university student,

1678
01:40:24,217 --> 01:40:25,551
someone you know
is not a spy,

1679
01:40:25,586 --> 01:40:27,087
who's no threat to you.

1680
01:40:27,121 --> 01:40:28,555
And in exchange
for this person

1681
01:40:28,588 --> 01:40:30,323
who is worthless to you,

1682
01:40:30,356 --> 01:40:32,525
you play an equal part
in an exchange

1683
01:40:32,559 --> 01:40:34,627
between the Russians
and the Americans.

1684
01:40:34,661 --> 01:40:39,166
And this is one transaction
between us and the two of you.

1685
01:40:39,198 --> 01:40:40,634
We're not trying to do
two things here, sir.

1686
01:40:40,667 --> 01:40:42,368
We're just doing one thing.
One, one, one.

1687
01:40:42,403 --> 01:40:47,474
It's hard for me to see how the Republic of
East German Democrats is being slighted.

1688
01:40:47,507 --> 01:40:50,377
You agreed with the Soviets
without consulting me.

1689
01:40:50,411 --> 01:40:52,345
Is this a negotiation
or a conspiracy?

1690
01:40:52,378 --> 01:40:53,279
For it to be
a conspiracy,

1691
01:40:53,313 --> 01:40:54,480
there would have to be
harm to you, sir.

1692
01:40:54,515 --> 01:40:57,451
There's just benefits here.
We're of agreeing interests.

1693
01:40:57,484 --> 01:40:59,420
No, you decide
they agree.

1694
01:41:00,421 --> 01:41:02,155
Where is your
appointment, sir?

1695
01:41:02,189 --> 01:41:03,590
In the West.
Ku'damm.

1696
01:41:03,623 --> 01:41:04,623
Good.

1697
01:41:15,769 --> 01:41:16,936
<font color="#ffff00">VOGEL</font>: Look around you.

1698
01:41:16,970 --> 01:41:20,474
How does the Eastern sector
compare with the West? Hmm?

1699
01:41:20,506 --> 01:41:22,475
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1700
01:41:22,509 --> 01:41:24,478
Our Russian friends
have decided that

1701
01:41:24,511 --> 01:41:26,447
we should not rebuild
our capital city.

1702
01:41:28,715 --> 01:41:32,219
But we live in this ruin
made by our Russian friends.

1703
01:41:32,252 --> 01:41:34,220
Go ahead, make your deal
with these Russians.

1704
01:41:34,253 --> 01:41:35,421
We won't be part of it.

1705
01:41:37,524 --> 01:41:38,725
There is no deal
without Pryor.

1706
01:41:38,759 --> 01:41:39,725
We are not
leaving him here.

1707
01:41:39,760 --> 01:41:41,761
Is this your position
or your government's?

1708
01:41:41,795 --> 01:41:45,565
Sir, I'm here.
I'm talking to you.

1709
01:41:45,599 --> 01:41:47,533
But you are not a representative
of your government.

1710
01:41:47,568 --> 01:41:50,270
You don't know who you are.
Neither do we.

1711
01:41:50,304 --> 01:41:52,605
You should be careful.
This is not Brooklyn, Mr. Donovan.

1712
01:41:58,545 --> 01:41:59,545
<font color="#80FF00">(HORN HONKS)</font>

1713
01:42:00,279 --> 01:42:01,681
Look, sir.

1714
01:42:01,715 --> 01:42:05,750
You must know that
in every respect that matters,
I represent
my government.

1716
01:42:05,786 --> 01:42:07,287
You know who I am.

1717
01:42:08,421 --> 01:42:09,456
Do I?

1718
01:42:11,257 --> 01:42:12,626
<font color="#80FF00">(SIREN WAILING)</font>

1719
01:42:15,429 --> 01:42:17,230
<font color="#80FF00">(SIGHS)</font> Uh-oh.

1720
01:42:31,545 --> 01:42:34,381
Do you have
the proper papers?

1721
01:42:34,413 --> 01:42:37,283
Of course not.
It doesn't matter.

1722
01:42:37,317 --> 01:42:38,484
An American lawyer
like you

1723
01:42:38,519 --> 01:42:40,287
can talk your way
out of anything.

1724
01:42:40,319 --> 01:42:41,521
Can't you?

1725
01:42:48,261 --> 01:42:49,462
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN GERMAN)</font>

1726
01:43:24,331 --> 01:43:26,265
You go with him.

1727
01:43:26,300 --> 01:43:28,801
Why?
It's passport problem.

1728
01:43:28,835 --> 01:43:30,404
What... What problem?
What's the problem?

1729
01:43:30,436 --> 01:43:31,637
You go with him.

1730
01:43:32,506 --> 01:43:33,606
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1731
01:43:34,541 --> 01:43:35,876
And remember,

1732
01:43:35,908 --> 01:43:37,677
we control the fate
of Frederic Pryor.

1733
01:43:59,666 --> 01:44:01,467
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)</font>

1734
01:44:22,889 --> 01:44:24,625
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1735
01:44:40,506 --> 01:44:42,609
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

1736
01:44:50,951 --> 01:44:51,951
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLES)</font>

1737
01:45:10,569 --> 01:45:11,569
<font color="#80FF00">(GUNFIRE)</font>

1738
01:45:12,905 --> 01:45:14,507
<font color="#80FF00">(ALL GRUNTING)</font>

1739
01:45:21,448 --> 01:45:22,515
<font color="#80FF00">(ALL GASPING)</font>

1740
01:45:22,548 --> 01:45:23,684
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: My God!

1741
01:45:24,618 --> 01:45:26,386
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

1742
01:45:57,917 --> 01:45:59,185
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1743
01:45:59,219 --> 01:46:01,654
<font color="#80FF00">(CLEARS THROAT)</font> Yeah,
I'll start with some coffee, please.

1744
01:46:01,688 --> 01:46:03,856
Then I'll have
the Hilton combo

1745
01:46:03,890 --> 01:46:05,725
and the American
breakfast.

1746
01:46:05,759 --> 01:46:06,859
And coffee.

1747
01:46:06,893 --> 01:46:07,961
Both breakfasts?

1748
01:46:07,993 --> 01:46:09,529
Yeah, yeah.

1749
01:46:09,563 --> 01:46:10,764
One first or...

1750
01:46:10,796 --> 01:46:12,498
Both first, both.
Whenever they're ready.

1751
01:46:12,532 --> 01:46:14,501
And coffee.
Hmm.

1752
01:46:32,085 --> 01:46:33,854
You shouldn't be here.

1753
01:46:33,886 --> 01:46:35,055
Well, sometimes
in Germany,

1754
01:46:35,087 --> 01:46:36,957
you just want a big
American breakfast.

1755
01:46:41,027 --> 01:46:43,063
What happened?
Where were you last night?

1756
01:46:43,095 --> 01:46:46,065
Vogel arranged for me
to spend some time in the East.

1757
01:46:46,099 --> 01:46:47,667
Oh, Jesus.

1758
01:46:47,701 --> 01:46:48,935
Frankly, it's not
that much worse

1759
01:46:48,969 --> 01:46:50,804
than where you have me
here in the West.

1760
01:46:52,872 --> 01:46:56,542
So the Russians are fine
with the exchange,

1761
01:46:56,576 --> 01:46:59,078
but now Vogel is saying
that the East Germans won't do it.

1762
01:46:59,112 --> 01:47:00,112
We're not gonna
get Pryor.

1763
01:47:01,515 --> 01:47:03,516
Great, good.

1764
01:47:03,550 --> 01:47:05,551
So the Soviets are set.
We get Powers.

1765
01:47:05,585 --> 01:47:06,853
We get Powers.

1766
01:47:06,886 --> 01:47:08,889
Well done.

1767
01:47:08,921 --> 01:47:10,957
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: No, the East Germans
won't do it.

1768
01:47:10,990 --> 01:47:13,092
We won't get Pryor,
and that kid matters.

1769
01:47:13,126 --> 01:47:14,828
Every person matters.

1770
01:47:14,860 --> 01:47:17,898
Sure, that's why you tried.
That's why you tried.

1771
01:47:17,930 --> 01:47:19,966
Abel's on his way,
so we're all set for tomorrow morning.

1772
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:22,569
Wait a minute.
Makes things simple. Makes things simple.

1773
01:47:22,601 --> 01:47:24,871
We are not
getting the kid.

1774
01:47:24,904 --> 01:47:27,039
Yeah, I understand.
We're set.

1775
01:47:27,073 --> 01:47:28,872
We can ignore the message
we got this morning.

1776
01:47:30,676 --> 01:47:31,912
What message?

1777
01:47:31,944 --> 01:47:33,078
From the East Germans.

1778
01:47:33,113 --> 01:47:35,047
They called the number
I gave you.

1779
01:47:35,081 --> 01:47:37,583
They said they wanted
to speak to you today.

1780
01:47:37,617 --> 01:47:39,586
Vogel called?
No, um...

1781
01:47:39,618 --> 01:47:41,954
Office of, uh,
Harald Ott,

1782
01:47:41,988 --> 01:47:43,556
East German
attorney general.

1783
01:47:43,590 --> 01:47:45,057
Wanting what?
What does he want?

1784
01:47:45,091 --> 01:47:46,193
Well, to talk to you.

1785
01:47:46,225 --> 01:47:49,128
But it's okay,
I can't ask you to go back there.

1786
01:47:49,162 --> 01:47:51,765
And listen, you seem to be
persona non grata to some elements,

1787
01:47:51,797 --> 01:47:53,099
so we won't
push our luck.

1788
01:47:53,132 --> 01:47:55,135
So he wants to see me.

1789
01:47:55,167 --> 01:47:57,103
What time?

1790
01:47:57,136 --> 01:48:00,073
Oh, no, no. Now, listen.
<font color="#80FF00">(STUTTERING)</font>

1791
01:48:00,105 --> 01:48:02,141
You don't need to go.
In fact, you shouldn't go.

1792
01:48:02,175 --> 01:48:04,610
In fact, you can't go.

1793
01:48:04,644 --> 01:48:06,979
We're set with the Soviets.
We don't want to screw it up.

1794
01:48:07,012 --> 01:48:08,948
I'm not gonna screw it up.
You're not going!

1795
01:48:08,981 --> 01:48:10,550
Don't be a pain in the ass.

1796
01:48:10,583 --> 01:48:11,952
Oh, I ordered.

1797
01:48:11,984 --> 01:48:14,054
Enjoy your big
American breakfast.

1798
01:48:20,126 --> 01:48:21,126
<font color="#80FF00">(SIGHS)</font>

1799
01:48:23,796 --> 01:48:25,731
I thought it should
be negotiated

1800
01:48:25,765 --> 01:48:27,967
at the highest level
as a courtesy to you.

1801
01:48:28,001 --> 01:48:29,802
I thank you, sir.

1802
01:48:32,037 --> 01:48:34,908
I don't know how things
became so confused.

1803
01:48:34,940 --> 01:48:37,743
Well, I guess sometimes
they just do.

1804
01:48:42,215 --> 01:48:43,817
For sake of clarity,

1805
01:48:43,850 --> 01:48:46,185
I say we are fully
prepared to exchange

1806
01:48:46,219 --> 01:48:50,923
the so-called student, Pryor,
for Rudolf Abel.

1807
01:48:50,957 --> 01:48:53,792
I understand from Mr. Vogel
that Pryor is very important to you.

1808
01:48:53,827 --> 01:48:55,194
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Yes, but...

1809
01:48:55,228 --> 01:48:57,796
Ah, we'll do a memorandum
to that effect.

1810
01:48:59,164 --> 01:49:01,100
Oh, a memorandum.

1811
01:49:01,134 --> 01:49:04,604
Certainly, we commit
to a bi-lateral exchange.

1812
01:49:06,139 --> 01:49:10,676
Yes, yes, sure. I'm not sure
what that means but...

1813
01:49:10,710 --> 01:49:15,081
My country also insists
on Francis Gary Powers.

1814
01:49:15,114 --> 01:49:16,181
No!

1815
01:49:18,717 --> 01:49:20,052
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1816
01:49:21,253 --> 01:49:24,925
This is how things
became so confused.

1817
01:49:24,957 --> 01:49:27,694
Powers. What use
is he, sir?

1818
01:49:27,726 --> 01:49:29,628
You want him back for
punitive reasons?

1819
01:49:29,662 --> 01:49:30,863
What's done is done.

1820
01:49:30,896 --> 01:49:32,198
He has divulged
what he will divulge,

1821
01:49:32,231 --> 01:49:35,034
as no doubt
Abel has also.

1822
01:49:35,068 --> 01:49:39,838
We are offering instead
someone who you insist is innocent.

1823
01:49:39,872 --> 01:49:41,173
And this is right.

1824
01:49:41,207 --> 01:49:42,841
This is who
you should retrieve.

1825
01:49:43,275 --> 01:49:44,911
The future.

1826
01:49:44,944 --> 01:49:46,305
Look to the future.
<font color="#80FF00">(PHONE RINGING)</font>

1827
01:49:49,882 --> 01:49:50,951
Ott.
<font color="#80FF00">(RINGING CONTINUES)</font>

1828
01:49:53,787 --> 01:49:55,788
Ott.
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1829
01:50:04,163 --> 01:50:06,166
I'm sorry. If you could
give me a moment, sir.

1830
01:50:11,805 --> 01:50:13,206
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font>

1831
01:50:17,877 --> 01:50:19,045
<font color="#80FF00">(BELLS RINGING)</font>

1832
01:50:26,152 --> 01:50:28,287
I'm very sorry, sir.
The attorney general regrets

1833
01:50:28,320 --> 01:50:30,990
that he had to leave
on urgent business.

1834
01:50:31,024 --> 01:50:34,260
He left?
Yes. Many apologies.

1835
01:50:34,293 --> 01:50:36,695
I've been waiting
for over an hour.

1836
01:50:36,729 --> 01:50:38,064
Very sorry, sir.

1837
01:50:40,300 --> 01:50:41,801
Young man.

1838
01:50:43,936 --> 01:50:45,037
Come here.

1839
01:50:46,805 --> 01:50:48,240
It's all right.
Come here.

1840
01:50:53,779 --> 01:50:56,015
Uh...
Take a seat.

1841
01:50:58,083 --> 01:51:00,052
Oh, come on, come on.
Sit down.

1842
01:51:04,224 --> 01:51:05,958
Do you like
your job here?

1843
01:51:06,826 --> 01:51:08,194
It's a very
good job, sir.

1844
01:51:08,228 --> 01:51:10,329
It must be.
Must be interesting.

1845
01:51:10,363 --> 01:51:11,797
Oh, yes, sir.

1846
01:51:11,831 --> 01:51:13,932
And important, too.

1847
01:51:13,966 --> 01:51:16,935
And your English is...
Is good.

1848
01:51:16,970 --> 01:51:18,971
Yes, I hesitate to say
it's excellent.

1849
01:51:19,005 --> 01:51:19,872
But it's excellent,
sir.

1850
01:51:19,905 --> 01:51:21,940
Good, good.
Good.

1851
01:51:21,975 --> 01:51:24,877
See, I just lost my
negotiating partner,

1852
01:51:24,911 --> 01:51:26,945
and I need
somebody to talk to,

1853
01:51:26,979 --> 01:51:28,914
and you seem like
a reasonable young man.

1854
01:51:30,216 --> 01:51:31,850
Can I talk to you?

1855
01:51:33,185 --> 01:51:34,185
Uh...

1856
01:51:35,020 --> 01:51:36,256
It's all right.

1857
01:51:36,288 --> 01:51:38,057
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>
It's all right. I just...

1858
01:51:40,159 --> 01:51:42,228
I just need you to give
a message to your boss.

1859
01:51:44,797 --> 01:51:46,358
But it has to be
very, very, very clear.

1860
01:51:47,833 --> 01:51:48,902
You understand?

1861
01:51:50,170 --> 01:51:53,305
Yes, sir. But perhaps
you should wait until he...

1862
01:51:53,338 --> 01:51:56,810
No, no. The thing is,
I have this cold and I wanna get home

1863
01:51:57,342 --> 01:51:59,212
and get in bed.

1864
01:51:59,244 --> 01:52:00,947
<font color="#80FF00">(BLOWS NOSE
AND CLEARS THROAT)</font>

1865
01:52:00,979 --> 01:52:02,381
Can you give him
the message?

1866
01:52:02,414 --> 01:52:03,817
Certainly.

1867
01:52:05,819 --> 01:52:07,087
Well, this is
the message.

1868
01:52:09,154 --> 01:52:11,824
"There is no deal for
Abel unless we get Powers and Pryor."

1869
01:52:13,359 --> 01:52:15,828
Do you understand?
Yes, sir.

1870
01:52:15,862 --> 01:52:17,029
It's arranged for
tomorrow morning.

1871
01:52:17,063 --> 01:52:19,399
It will not happen
unless we get two men.

1872
01:52:19,431 --> 01:52:21,134
Those two men.

1873
01:52:21,167 --> 01:52:22,836
Two, two, two.
Yes, sir.

1874
01:52:23,435 --> 01:52:26,206
If there is no deal,

1875
01:52:26,238 --> 01:52:27,873
your boss must tell
the Soviets.

1876
01:52:29,408 --> 01:52:31,144
He has to tell
the Soviets

1877
01:52:31,176 --> 01:52:33,212
that they are not
getting Rudolf Abel.

1878
01:52:35,414 --> 01:52:37,117
Yes, sir.

1879
01:52:40,052 --> 01:52:41,320
Oh, and tell him this.

1880
01:52:43,088 --> 01:52:46,059
That so far, Abel has been
a good soldier,

1881
01:52:46,091 --> 01:52:48,161
but he thinks
he's going home.

1882
01:52:48,194 --> 01:52:50,329
<font color="#80FF00">(COUGHS)</font>

1883
01:52:50,363 --> 01:52:52,966
If we had to tell him
that he's not going home,

1884
01:52:52,998 --> 01:52:54,266
that the Soviets
don't even want him,

1885
01:52:54,300 --> 01:52:55,902
that he's never
going home...

1886
01:52:55,935 --> 01:52:58,070
Well, I imagine his
behavior might change.

1887
01:53:00,140 --> 01:53:03,009
And who will be held
responsible for that?

1888
01:53:05,078 --> 01:53:06,845
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1889
01:53:06,880 --> 01:53:08,081
That's a long message.

1890
01:53:08,113 --> 01:53:10,149
Uh, did you get it all?

1891
01:53:10,182 --> 01:53:12,351
Yes, sir. Got it.
Good.

1892
01:53:14,020 --> 01:53:15,154
You're a good man.

1893
01:53:16,221 --> 01:53:17,222
Oh...

1894
01:53:18,123 --> 01:53:19,859
And also tell him

1895
01:53:19,893 --> 01:53:21,060
there's no deal
unless we hear

1896
01:53:21,094 --> 01:53:22,929
before the end of
business today.

1897
01:53:22,962 --> 01:53:24,229
He has the number.

1898
01:53:24,264 --> 01:53:26,265
If the exchange
isn't gonna happen,

1899
01:53:26,298 --> 01:53:28,333
no reason for everybody
to get up first thing in the morning.

1900
01:53:28,368 --> 01:53:32,070
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font> No, sir,
that would be pointless.

1901
01:53:32,104 --> 01:53:34,908
You're kidding me!
You're kidding me!

1902
01:53:34,940 --> 01:53:37,109
Not really.
I need some change.

1903
01:53:37,143 --> 01:53:39,378
This is exactly
what you weren't supposed to do.

1904
01:53:39,412 --> 01:53:42,215
My instructions were
to feel out the situation.

1905
01:53:42,247 --> 01:53:45,051
Your instructions
were to get Powers, not Pryor.

1906
01:53:45,083 --> 01:53:47,319
That was not the original deal.
You fouled it all up!

1907
01:53:47,353 --> 01:53:49,988
How do you know?
I feel pretty good about it.

1908
01:53:50,023 --> 01:53:51,390
You fucked it all up!

1909
01:53:51,423 --> 01:53:53,225
Oh, don't worry.

1910
01:53:53,259 --> 01:53:55,360
I think it's
gonna be fine.

1911
01:53:55,395 --> 01:53:57,130
How do you know?
You don't know that.

1912
01:53:58,497 --> 01:54:01,234
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING GERMAN)</font>

1913
01:54:01,266 --> 01:54:03,368
United States.
New York, New York.

1914
01:54:03,403 --> 01:54:04,938
<i>Danke.</i>

1915
01:54:06,005 --> 01:54:08,975
Look, this whole thing

1916
01:54:09,007 --> 01:54:11,911
has been to feel me out.

1917
01:54:11,944 --> 01:54:14,247
Would I swap one for one
and which one?

1918
01:54:15,314 --> 01:54:17,283
But I said
"No, two for one."

1919
01:54:17,317 --> 01:54:19,252
So they know
where we stand.

1920
01:54:19,284 --> 01:54:21,320
Yeah, what if they think
about it and say, uh...

1921
01:54:21,354 --> 01:54:23,488
"Forget it! You keep our guy,
we keep your guys"?

1922
01:54:26,426 --> 01:54:28,528
Well, then I have
fucked it all up.

1923
01:54:28,560 --> 01:54:31,064
Hey, honey! It's me.

1924
01:54:31,096 --> 01:54:33,966
Yeah. The fishing's
been great.

1925
01:54:35,969 --> 01:54:37,302
I'm in London,

1926
01:54:37,337 --> 01:54:39,337
just for one more meeting,
then I'm on my way home.

1927
01:54:40,573 --> 01:54:44,042
Yeah, yeah, I remember
that marmalade.

1928
01:54:44,077 --> 01:54:46,179
It's that shop
right by Regent's Park.

1929
01:54:47,479 --> 01:54:49,414
All right, I will
if I have the time.

1930
01:54:49,448 --> 01:54:51,216
Hey, can I say
hi to Roger?

1931
01:54:52,452 --> 01:54:54,119
Could I say hi
to the girls?

1932
01:54:55,054 --> 01:54:56,189
Is anyone not busy?

1933
01:54:57,422 --> 01:54:59,926
Have they even noticed
I'm not around?

1934
01:55:20,345 --> 01:55:21,546
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

1935
01:55:29,622 --> 01:55:31,256
<font color="#80FF00">(PHONE RINGING)</font>

1936
01:55:56,215 --> 01:55:57,350
We're on.

1937
01:55:58,451 --> 01:56:00,052
Two for one.

1938
01:56:01,119 --> 01:56:03,088
Hot dog.
<font color="#80FF00">(ALL LAUGH)</font>

1939
01:56:03,122 --> 01:56:05,257
Let's have a drink.

1940
01:56:05,291 --> 01:56:08,027
One wrinkle.
What is that?

1941
01:56:08,060 --> 01:56:10,529
They won't release
Pryor with Powers, not at the bridge.

1942
01:56:10,563 --> 01:56:12,297
They'll release him
at the same time.

1943
01:56:12,332 --> 01:56:14,434
When the Russians give us
Powers on the bridge,

1944
01:56:14,466 --> 01:56:17,103
they'll release Pryor
over Checkpoint Charlie.

1945
01:56:17,136 --> 01:56:18,637
What does that mean?

1946
01:56:18,671 --> 01:56:22,140
I think it means,
"We wanna have the last word."

1947
01:56:23,176 --> 01:56:24,176
But they will do it.

1948
01:56:25,278 --> 01:56:26,479
You tell me.
You felt them out.

1949
01:56:47,567 --> 01:56:49,167
Where are they?

1950
01:56:49,202 --> 01:56:50,536
Where's our guy?
Where's our guy?

1951
01:56:50,569 --> 01:56:52,171
They're late.

1952
01:56:52,204 --> 01:56:54,140
Nobody's late.
We're early.

1953
01:56:54,172 --> 01:56:55,608
May I?
Yeah.

1954
01:56:56,442 --> 01:56:58,243
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

1955
01:57:02,582 --> 01:57:03,649
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Hoffman.

1956
01:57:03,682 --> 01:57:05,217
<font color="#ffff00">HOFFMAN</font>: Yeah?

1957
01:57:05,251 --> 01:57:06,685
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: I think they have...

1958
01:57:06,719 --> 01:57:08,453
<font color="#ffff00">HOFFMAN</font>: What?

1959
01:57:08,488 --> 01:57:10,355
Snipers.

1960
01:57:10,390 --> 01:57:11,657
<font color="#ffff00">HOFFMAN</font>: Well,
I'm sure they do.

1961
01:57:12,557 --> 01:57:14,260
How are you
sure they do?

1962
01:57:14,726 --> 01:57:16,529
Because we have snipers.

1963
01:57:34,346 --> 01:57:35,480
Step out, sir.

1964
01:57:43,756 --> 01:57:45,190
Jim.

1965
01:57:49,429 --> 01:57:50,429
Dear Jim.

1966
01:57:52,465 --> 01:57:54,367
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>
How are you?

1967
01:57:55,234 --> 01:57:57,403
Happy to see you.

1968
01:57:57,436 --> 01:57:59,372
Have you arranged
all this for me?

1969
01:57:59,404 --> 01:58:02,207
Well, let's see what this is before I...
I take credit for it.

1970
01:58:03,509 --> 01:58:05,444
Any word about Pryor
from Checkpoint Charlie?

1971
01:58:07,512 --> 01:58:08,747
<font color="#80FF00">(DOG BARKING)</font>

1972
01:58:08,780 --> 01:58:10,716
<font color="#80FF00">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING
IN GERMAN)</font>

1973
01:58:29,801 --> 01:58:31,503
Nothing cooking yet.

1974
01:58:38,243 --> 01:58:39,545
Excuse me, sir.

1975
01:58:39,578 --> 01:58:41,713
This is Lieutenant Joe Murphy,
United States Air Force,

1976
01:58:41,747 --> 01:58:43,483
here to identify Powers.

1977
01:58:44,250 --> 01:58:45,318
Lieutenant Murphy.

1978
01:58:45,751 --> 01:58:47,252
Sir.

1979
01:58:50,422 --> 01:58:52,225
There may be
a slight glitch.

1980
01:58:52,257 --> 01:58:54,393
I was a young man
when I left.

1981
01:58:54,426 --> 01:58:56,462
Who will they find
to identify me?

1982
01:58:57,697 --> 01:59:00,432
Well, I hope it's not your
East German family.

1983
01:59:00,466 --> 01:59:02,301
I doubt they could
identify each other.

1984
01:59:02,702 --> 01:59:03,702
<font color="#80FF00">(WHISTLING)</font>

1985
01:59:37,470 --> 01:59:38,671
Looks like our party.

1986
01:59:46,511 --> 01:59:48,814
Well, let's go.

1987
01:59:48,847 --> 01:59:50,515
You can stay here,
Donovan.

1988
01:59:51,650 --> 01:59:52,784
Not likely.

1989
01:59:55,354 --> 01:59:56,521
Open the gate.

1990
02:00:11,570 --> 02:00:13,538
What do you think will happen
when you get home?

1991
02:00:14,306 --> 02:00:15,608
Uh, I think...

1992
02:00:16,641 --> 02:00:18,578
I'll have a vodka.
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

1993
02:00:21,413 --> 02:00:22,480
Yeah.

1994
02:00:23,381 --> 02:00:24,649
Yeah.

1995
02:00:24,684 --> 02:00:27,485
But, Rudolf, is there
not the possibility...

1996
02:00:27,520 --> 02:00:29,588
That my people
are going to shoot me?

1997
02:00:30,789 --> 02:00:31,823
Yes.

1998
02:00:33,626 --> 02:00:35,694
You're not worried?
Would it help?

1999
02:00:47,840 --> 02:00:51,230
To answer your question,
my friend, I acted honorably.

2000
02:00:51,242 --> 02:00:53,412
I think they know that.

2001
02:00:54,713 --> 02:00:56,582
But sometimes
people think wrong.

2002
02:00:56,615 --> 02:00:57,817
People are people.

2003
02:00:59,551 --> 02:01:00,886
Let's see how they
greet me.

2004
02:01:02,755 --> 02:01:04,390
What can I look for?

2005
02:01:07,359 --> 02:01:10,863
If I'm embraced or just
shown the back seat.

2006
02:01:37,622 --> 02:01:39,391
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING IN OTHER LANGUAGE)</font>

2007
02:01:51,604 --> 02:01:52,738
<font color="#ffff00">ABEL</font>:
<i>Da.</i>

2008
02:01:52,771 --> 02:01:55,507
<font color="#80FF00">(CONVERSING IN RUSSIAN)</font>

2009
02:02:05,551 --> 02:02:06,918
Take off the hat.

2010
02:02:13,492 --> 02:02:14,694
Hey, Powers.

2011
02:02:16,494 --> 02:02:17,762
Hey, Murph.

2012
02:02:19,631 --> 02:02:20,865
Yeah, that's Powers.

2013
02:02:23,703 --> 02:02:25,904
<font color="#ffff00">SCHISCHKIN</font>: All right,
Mr. Donovan. We go now!

2014
02:02:25,938 --> 02:02:27,405
Hang on.

2015
02:02:34,680 --> 02:02:35,980
Believe me,
I will tell you.

2016
02:02:38,050 --> 02:02:39,585
There's no one here.

2017
02:02:42,921 --> 02:02:44,989
They're releasing another man
at Checkpoint Charlie.

2018
02:02:45,024 --> 02:02:47,025
We're just confirming
that he's there.

2019
02:02:47,059 --> 02:02:48,661
<font color="#ffff00">SCHISCHKIN</font>: Now!

2020
02:02:48,694 --> 02:02:49,994
As per plan,
if you please.

2021
02:02:51,596 --> 02:02:53,032
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font> Let's go.

2022
02:02:53,064 --> 02:02:55,900
Pryor will show up
or he won't. Let's go.

2023
02:02:55,935 --> 02:02:58,804
They're waiting to see
if we'll do it without him.

2024
02:02:58,837 --> 02:03:00,739
We just have to stand here,
show them we won't.

2025
02:03:02,541 --> 02:03:03,676
I don't give a shit
what they want.

2026
02:03:03,708 --> 02:03:06,011
We have our objective.
Go ahead, Abel.

2027
02:03:07,813 --> 02:03:10,682
Go ahead, sir.
You can go.

2028
02:03:10,716 --> 02:03:12,784
If it is not to plan,
we go home.

2029
02:03:12,818 --> 02:03:14,753
We exchange now
or we go home!

2030
02:03:17,555 --> 02:03:19,759
You're waiting for another man?
Yup.

2031
02:03:20,826 --> 02:03:22,427
You want him, too.

2032
02:03:24,497 --> 02:03:25,730
I want him.

2033
02:03:25,765 --> 02:03:26,931
Doesn't matter
what he wants, sir.

2034
02:03:26,966 --> 02:03:28,768
I'm in charge and
you are free to go.

2035
02:03:29,702 --> 02:03:31,470
Please go.
Walk across.

2036
02:03:33,706 --> 02:03:34,907
<font color="#80FF00">(SPEAKING RUSSIAN)</font>

2037
02:03:38,109 --> 02:03:39,744
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES SOFTLY)</font>

2038
02:03:43,114 --> 02:03:44,516
I can wait.

2039
02:04:09,608 --> 02:04:11,976
It's something.
I'm not sure yet.

2040
02:04:14,046 --> 02:04:15,079
Hang on!

2041
02:05:00,059 --> 02:05:01,059
<font color="#80FF00">(WHISPERS)</font> Let's go.

2042
02:05:07,733 --> 02:05:08,833
We got him!

2043
02:05:09,935 --> 02:05:11,103
Uh, I, uh...

2044
02:05:13,738 --> 02:05:15,573
I sent you a gift, Jim.

2045
02:05:16,675 --> 02:05:18,143
It's a...
It's a painting.

2046
02:05:19,878 --> 02:05:21,913
I hope it has some
meaning to you.

2047
02:05:24,149 --> 02:05:26,118
I'm sorry. I didn't think
to get you a gift.

2048
02:05:29,889 --> 02:05:31,756
This is your gift.

2049
02:05:35,094 --> 02:05:36,628
This is your gift.

2050
02:05:41,233 --> 02:05:42,701
<font color="#80FF00">(SNIFFLING)</font>

2051
02:06:12,030 --> 02:06:13,030
You okay?

2052
02:06:14,867 --> 02:06:15,987
Let's go, let's go,
let's go!

2053
02:07:35,948 --> 02:07:37,916
Mr. Donovan.

2054
02:07:37,949 --> 02:07:39,885
Colonel Abel asked me
to give this to you.

2055
02:07:42,820 --> 02:07:43,956
Pardon me, sir.

2056
02:07:45,791 --> 02:07:47,270
Were you in charge
of this thing, sir?

2057
02:07:48,761 --> 02:07:50,895
Yeah.
Well, I gotta thank you.

2058
02:07:51,796 --> 02:07:53,032
I gotta thank somebody.

2059
02:08:10,248 --> 02:08:13,051
I gave them nothing.
I gave them nothing.

2060
02:08:14,252 --> 02:08:16,188
It doesn't matter.

2061
02:08:16,220 --> 02:08:18,823
It doesn't matter
what people think.

2062
02:08:18,856 --> 02:08:19,925
You know
what you did.

2063
02:09:09,307 --> 02:09:10,908
<font color="#80FF00">(BOTH EXCLAIMING)</font>

2064
02:09:10,943 --> 02:09:12,110
Welcome home.

2065
02:09:12,144 --> 02:09:13,212
<font color="#80FF00">(SIGHS IN RELIEF)</font>

2066
02:09:14,345 --> 02:09:15,913
What a trip.

2067
02:09:19,917 --> 02:09:20,953
<font color="#80FF00">(KISSES)</font>

2068
02:09:20,985 --> 02:09:22,154
<font color="#ffff00">MARY</font>: Mmm.

2069
02:09:25,423 --> 02:09:26,925
<font color="#ffff00">JAMES</font>: Mmm.

2070
02:09:27,359 --> 02:09:28,961
You okay?

2071
02:09:29,394 --> 02:09:31,063
Yeah, I'm fine.

2072
02:09:32,997 --> 02:09:35,000
Did you get the marmalade?
Yes, yes.

2073
02:09:35,033 --> 02:09:37,069
Yep. Yep.

2074
02:09:37,935 --> 02:09:39,203
I did.

2075
02:09:40,905 --> 02:09:42,106
As ordered.
<font color="#80FF00">(CHUCKLES)</font>

2076
02:09:46,278 --> 02:09:47,279
<font color="#80FF00">(GASPS)</font> Jim.

2077
02:09:48,380 --> 02:09:50,448
This is from Arno's.
So?

2078
02:09:50,481 --> 02:09:52,984
On the corner!
Well, honey, I was busy.

2079
02:09:53,018 --> 02:09:54,953
I'm sorry. We were so busy.
Oh, for Pete's sake.

2080
02:09:54,987 --> 02:09:56,387
How's Roger? Is he home?
He's home.

2081
02:09:56,421 --> 02:09:58,923
Carol's home, Peggy's home.
Everyone's home.

2082
02:09:58,957 --> 02:10:01,092
<font color="#ffff00">CAROL</font>: Mom!
Come quick! Look!

2083
02:10:02,360 --> 02:10:04,395
Mom, come here!
<font color="#ffff00">PEGGY</font>: Mom, come here!

2084
02:10:04,429 --> 02:10:06,965
<font color="#ffff00">ROGER</font>: Come see this! Look! Watch!
<font color="#ffff00">PEGGY</font>: Come on!

2085
02:10:06,998 --> 02:10:09,301
<i>Francis Gary Powers</i>

2086
02:10:09,333 --> 02:10:13,271
<i>has been released from prison
in the Soviet Union</i>

2087
02:10:13,305 --> 02:10:15,373
<i>and turned over to
American authorities</i>

2088
02:10:15,407 --> 02:10:18,277
<i>early this morning
in Berlin.</i>

2089
02:10:18,310 --> 02:10:23,081
<i>The President has commuted
the sentence of Rudolf Abel.</i>

2090
02:10:23,114 --> 02:10:25,984
<i>Mr. Abel has been
deported</i>

2091
02:10:26,018 --> 02:10:28,453
<i>and has been released
in Berlin.</i>

2092
02:10:28,487 --> 02:10:32,090
<i>Efforts to obtain
Mr. Powers' release</i>

2093
02:10:32,123 --> 02:10:34,926
<i>had been underway
for some time.</i>

2094
02:10:34,960 --> 02:10:36,461
<i>In recent efforts,</i>

2095
02:10:36,494 --> 02:10:38,497
<i>the United States
government has had</i>

2096
02:10:38,529 --> 02:10:43,034
<i>the cooperation and assistance
of Mr. James B. Donovan,</i>

2097
02:10:43,068 --> 02:10:45,369
<i>a New York attorney.</i>

2098
02:10:45,404 --> 02:10:49,006
<i>Frederic L. Pryor,
an American student</i>

2099
02:10:49,041 --> 02:10:51,909
<i>held by East German
authorities</i>

2100
02:10:51,944 --> 02:10:54,246
<i>since August of 1961...</i>

2101
02:10:54,279 --> 02:10:56,414
I thought Daddy
was fishing.

2102
02:10:56,448 --> 02:10:58,516
For salmon.

2103
02:10:58,550 --> 02:11:02,020
<i>Abel now has disappeared
into the communist world.</i>

2104
02:11:02,054 --> 02:11:04,288
<i>Powers is here,
answering questions.</i>

2105
02:11:04,323 --> 02:11:07,125
<i>And Donovan has gone back
to his law practice.</i>

2106
02:11:07,158 --> 02:11:09,293
<i>And now, here is
George Fenneman,</i>

2107
02:11:09,328 --> 02:11:11,395
<i>speaking for the Douglas Fir
Plywood Association.</i>

2108
02:13:27,728 --> 02:13:31,097
Following his return to Russia, 
Rudolf Abel was reunited with his wife and daughter.

2109
02:13:31,199 --> 02:13:34,899
He was never publicly acknowledged by the Soviet Union as a Spy.

2110
02:13:35,899 --> 02:13:39,399
Gary Powers died in a helicopter crash in 1977, 
while working for KNBC news.

2111
02:13:39,500 --> 02:13:45,399
He was posthumously awarded the CIA Director's Medal, 
and the USAF POW Medal in 2000 and the Silver Star in 2012.

2112
02:13:46,328 --> 02:13:50,398
In 1962, Frederic L. Pryor received his Ph.D. in Economics from Yale University.

2113
02:13:50,399 --> 02:13:53,699
He is currently Professor Emeritus of Economics, 
and Senior Research Scholar at Swarthmore College.

2114
02:13:54,899 --> 02:14:01,899
Following the successful conclusion of the Powers-Abel exchange, 
James Donovan was asked by President Kennedy to undertake 
further negotiations on behalf of the U.S.

2115
02:14:03,399 --> 02:14:10,199
In the summer of 1962, 
he was sent to Cuba to discuss with Fidel Castro 
the terms of the release of 1,113 prisoners 
held after the Bay of Pigs invasion.

2116
02:14:10,300 --> 02:14:15,899
When Donovan finished negotiations, 
he had secured the release of 9,703 men, women and children.

